    {
      id: "k4-5",
      open: true,
      topic: "Transfer and relever",
      points: 6,
      body: "<p>You work for Kystbygg AS, which is not listed, and must find its cost of capital. Nordvest Kraft ASA is listed, produces a very similar product and has comparable business risk. Nordvest maintains a debt-to-equity ratio of 1.0, its cost of equity is 15.5%, and the expected return on its debt is 4.7%. Kystbygg targets a debt-to-equity ratio of 0.5 and can borrow at an expected return of 4.1%. The corporate tax rate is 22%. The risk-free rate is 3.5% and the expected return on the market portfolio is 9.5%.</p><p>The formula sheet gives you the CAPM, the β<sub>U</sub> weighting, MM Proposition II and both WACC formulas. Remember the course convention: β<sub>U</sub> is weighted without taxes. Use 4 decimal places for rates.</p><p>(a) What is the unlevered cost of capital for Kystbygg? (2 points)</p><p>(b) What is Kystbygg's cost of equity? Show that the beta route and MM Proposition II agree. (2 points)</p><p>(c) What is Kystbygg's after-tax WACC? Explain in 2–3 sentences why Nordvest's leverage entered (a) but Kystbygg's own leverage did not. (2 points)</p>",
      solution: "<p><b>(a) Unlever Nordvest.</b> D/E = 1.0 means E/V = D/V = <b>0.5000</b>. The market risk premium is 0.095 − 0.035 = 0.06.<br>β<sub>E</sub> = (0.1550 − 0.0350)/0.0600 = <b>2.0000</b><br>β<sub>D</sub> = (0.0470 − 0.0350)/0.0600 = <b>0.2000</b>, not zero: the debt earns a premium over the risk-free rate, so it carries systematic risk<br>β<sub>U</sub> = (E/V)β<sub>E</sub> + (D/V)β<sub>D</sub> = 0.5000 × 2.0000 + 0.5000 × 0.2000 = 1.0000 + 0.1000 = <b>1.1000</b> (weighted without taxes)<br>r<sub>U</sub> = 0.0350 + 1.1000 × 0.0600 = 0.0350 + 0.0660 = <b>0.1010 (10.1000%)</b><br>Verification on rates: 0.5000 × 0.1550 + 0.5000 × 0.0470 = 0.0775 + 0.0235 = 0.1010 ✓ (an identity, since the CAPM is linear; it confirms the arithmetic).</p><p>Because the two firms share the same business risk, this is also Kystbygg's unlevered cost of capital: <b>r<sub>U</sub> = 10.1000%</b>. Kystbygg's own leverage plays no part in this answer.</p><p><b>(b) Relever to Kystbygg.</b> D/E = 0.5, so D/V = 0.5/1.5 = <b>0.3333</b> and E/V = <b>0.6667</b>. Kystbygg's own debt beta: β<sub>D</sub> = (0.0410 − 0.0350)/0.0600 = <b>0.1000</b>.</p><p>Beta route: β<sub>E</sub> = β<sub>U</sub> + (D/E)(β<sub>U</sub> − β<sub>D</sub>) = 1.1000 + 0.5 × (1.1000 − 0.1000) = 1.1000 + 0.5000 = <b>1.6000</b><br>r<sub>E</sub> = 0.0350 + 1.6000 × 0.0600 = 0.0350 + 0.0960 = <b>0.1310 (13.1000%)</b><br>MM II: r<sub>E</sub> = r<sub>U</sub> + (D/E)(r<sub>U</sub> − r<sub>D</sub>) = 0.1010 + 0.5 × (0.1010 − 0.0410) = 0.1010 + 0.0300 = <b>0.1310</b> ✓<br>The two agree because they are the same weighted average written in betas and in rates. Note that D/E = 0.5 goes into the relevering formula while the weights 0.6667 and 0.3333 go into the WACC; mixing them up is the standard slip.</p><p><b>(c) The WACC.</b><br>r<sub>wacc</sub> = (E/V)r<sub>E</sub> + (D/V)r<sub>D</sub>(1 − τ<sub>c</sub>) = 0.6667 × 0.1310 + 0.3333 × 0.0410 × 0.78 = 0.087333 + 0.010660 = 0.097993 = <b>0.0980 (9.7993%)</b><br>Shortcut: r<sub>U</sub> − (D/V)τ<sub>c</sub>r<sub>D</sub> = 0.1010 − 0.3333 × 0.22 × 0.0410 = 0.1010 − 0.003007 = 0.097993 ✓<br>Pre-tax: 0.087333 + 0.013667 = 0.1010 = r<sub>U</sub> ✓ (identity given MM II). Check 2: 0.6667 × 1.6000 + 0.3333 × 0.1000 = 1.066667 + 0.033333 = 1.1000 = β<sub>U</sub> ✓ (identity, since β<sub>E</sub> was relevered from this β<sub>U</sub>).</p><p><b>Why Nordvest's leverage entered (a) and Kystbygg's did not.</b> Nordvest's 15.5% is the return on a levered claim, so it reflects both the business risk and Nordvest's financing; unlevering with Nordvest's own weights strips the financing off and isolates what the assets alone require. That unlevered return is a property of the business, which the two firms share, and it does not depend on how either firm is financed. Kystbygg's leverage enters only when the asset risk is redistributed between its own debt and equity, in (b) and (c). H2024 Exercise 5(a) gave 6 of 20 points to exactly this transfer step, and its key notes that the target's own leverage plays no part in it.</p>",
      criteria: [
        "Unlevers Nordvest with Nordvest's own weights, 0.5000/0.5000 from D/E = 1.0, carrying β_D = 0.2000 (not zero, since 4.7% exceeds 3.5%): β_U = 1.0000 + 0.1000 = 1.1000 without taxes, r_U = 10.1000%.",
        "Transfers r_U to Kystbygg and explains why: r_U reflects business risk, which the two firms share; Nordvest's leverage was used only to strip its financing off, and Kystbygg's leverage plays no role in (a). H2024 Exercise 5(a) gave 6 of 20 points to this step.",
        "Keeps D/E = 0.5 in the relevering formula but converts it to D/V = 0.3333 and E/V = 0.6667 for the WACC weights. Plugging 0.3333 into MM II gives r_E = 12.1000%; using 0.5/0.5 as Kystbygg's weights gives a WACC of 8.1490%.",
        "Uses Kystbygg's own debt beta of 0.1000 from 4.1%, not Nordvest's 0.2000: β_E = 1.6000 and r_E = 13.1000%, with the beta route and MM II shown to agree.",
        "r_wacc = 9.7993% with (1 − τ_c) on r_D only, intermediate terms 0.087333 + 0.010660 written out; the pre-tax WACC returns 10.1000% and is labelled an identity given MM II. Every step shown, rates to 4 decimals."
      ]
    }
