window.EDU_DATA.chapterTasks[9] = {
  minutes: 72,
  tasks: [
    {
      id: "k9-1",
      open: true,
      topic: "Risk shifting and the agency cost",
      points: 10,
      body: "<p>Nordvik Marine AS has debt outstanding with face value K = 90, due in one year, and no other liabilities. The assets will be run as exactly one of two mutually exclusive strategies, S or R, and neither strategy requires new investment. Cash flow arrives in one year in one of two states. Investors are risk neutral and the one-year risk-free rate is r<sub>f</sub> = 5%. All figures in millions of NOK.</p><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>State</th><th>Probability</th><th>Cash flow under S</th><th>Cash flow under R</th></tr><tr><td>Boom</td><td class=\"n\">0.60</td><td class=\"n\">130</td><td class=\"n\">170</td></tr><tr><td>Bust</td><td class=\"n\">0.40</td><td class=\"n\">100</td><td class=\"n\">30</td></tr></table><p>(a) Which strategy would an all-equity financed Nordvik run, and what is the NPV of switching from that strategy to the other one? (2 points)</p><p>(b) With the debt in place, value debt and equity state by state under each strategy. Which strategy does management choose if it acts for the shareholders, and what is the mechanism called? (4 points)</p><p>(c) Quantify the agency cost of debt, and run consistency check 3 on the switch. (3 points)</p><p>(d) A classmate reports the agency cost as the amount the creditors lose. In one sentence, say why that is wrong. (1 point)</p>",
      solution: "<p><b>Setup.</b> Risk-neutral investors price every claim as its expected payoff discounted at r<sub>f</sub>, so each value is the expected payoff divided by 1.05. Dividing every figure by the same 1.05 changes no ranking, so you could compare expected payoffs and discount at the end, but the question asks for values, so discount.</p><p><b>(a) The first best.</b><br>E[CF<sub>S</sub>] = 0.60 × 130 + 0.40 × 100 = 78 + 40 = 118, so V<sub>S</sub> = 118/1.05 = <b>112.3810</b><br>E[CF<sub>R</sub>] = 0.60 × 170 + 0.40 × 30 = 102 + 12 = 114, so V<sub>R</sub> = 114/1.05 = <b>108.5714</b><br>An all-equity firm owns the whole cash flow and therefore runs <b>S</b>. The NPV of switching from S to R is V<sub>R</sub> − V<sub>S</sub> = 108.5714 − 112.3810 = <b>−3.8095</b>. R is a mean-reducing spread: it adds 40 in the boom and takes 70 out of the bust.</p><p><b>(b) The state-by-state split.</b> Creditors receive min(CF, K) and shareholders max(CF − K, 0), where K = 90. Limited liability is what puts the zero in the second expression: equity in R's bust state is max(30 − 90, 0) = 0, never −60.</p><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>State (prob.)</th><th>CF under S</th><th>Debt</th><th>Equity</th><th>CF under R</th><th>Debt</th><th>Equity</th></tr><tr><td>Boom (0.60)</td><td class=\"n\">130</td><td class=\"n\">90</td><td class=\"n\">40</td><td class=\"n\">170</td><td class=\"n\">90</td><td class=\"n\">80</td></tr><tr><td>Bust (0.40)</td><td class=\"n\">100</td><td class=\"n\">90</td><td class=\"n\">10</td><td class=\"n\">30</td><td class=\"n\">30</td><td class=\"n\">0</td></tr><tr><td><b>Expected payoff</b></td><td class=\"n\"><b>118</b></td><td class=\"n\"><b>90</b></td><td class=\"n\"><b>28</b></td><td class=\"n\"><b>114</b></td><td class=\"n\"><b>66</b></td><td class=\"n\"><b>48</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Value today (÷1.05)</b></td><td class=\"n\"><b>112.3810</b></td><td class=\"n\"><b>85.7143</b></td><td class=\"n\"><b>26.6667</b></td><td class=\"n\"><b>108.5714</b></td><td class=\"n\"><b>62.8571</b></td><td class=\"n\"><b>45.7143</b></td></tr></table><p>Arithmetic behind the row: expected debt payoff under R is 0.60 × 90 + 0.40 × 30 = 54 + 12 = 66, and expected equity payoff is 0.60 × 80 + 0.40 × 0 = 48. Under S the bust state still covers the full 90, so the debt is <b>risk free</b>: D<sub>S</sub> = 90/1.05 = 85.7143. Under R it is risky.</p><p><b>Check that the claims exhaust the firm, state by state and in value:</b> 85.7143 + 26.6667 = 112.3810 ✓ and 62.8571 + 45.7143 = 108.5714 ✓</p><p><b>The choice.</b> Management maximises the value of the shareholders' claim, and E<sub>R</sub> = 45.7143 exceeds E<sub>S</sub> = 26.6667, so <b>management runs R</b>, the strategy that destroys value. Name the mechanism: this is <b>risk shifting</b>, also called <b>asset substitution</b>. Equity is a call option on firm value struck at K = 90, and a call is convex, so a mean-preserving or even a mean-reducing spread can raise its value: shareholders collect the whole of the extra 40 in the boom, while the extra 70 of loss in the bust falls on creditors who were already not being paid in full there.</p><p><b>(c) The agency cost.</b><br>agency cost = V(first best) − V(chosen) = 112.3810 − 108.5714 = <b>3.8095</b><br>This is a deadweight loss, the value that disappears because the decision maximises equity rather than the firm.</p><p><b>Consistency check 3 (creditors' gain + shareholders' gain = the project's NPV):</b><br>creditors' gain = D<sub>R</sub> − D<sub>S</sub> = 62.8571 − 85.7143 = <b>−22.8571</b><br>shareholders' gain = E<sub>R</sub> − E<sub>S</sub> = 45.7143 − 26.6667 = <b>+19.0476</b><br>Sum: −22.8571 + 19.0476 = −3.8095 = the NPV of switching ✓ The check is worth running because it can fail: forget the max(·, 0) in R's bust state and the equity gain comes out too large, breaking the identity.</p><p><b>(d) Why the creditors' loss is not the agency cost.</b> Of the 22.8571 the creditors lose, 19.0476 is a pure transfer into the shareholders' pockets and only the remaining 3.8095 leaves the economy, so the agency cost is the destroyed value, not the transfer.</p><p><b>A word on who ends up paying.</b> If creditors foresee at issue that R will be run, they will pay 62.8571 for the bond rather than 85.7143, so the firm raises 22.8571 less today and the original owners end with 62.8571 + 45.7143 = 108.5714 against the 112.3810 they would have had if they could have promised S. The shareholders bear the 3.8095 ex ante. That is why they accept covenants.</p>",
      criteria: [
        "Computes V_S = 112.3810 and V_R = 108.5714 and states that an all-equity firm runs S, with the NPV of switching equal to −3.8095. Discounting at 1.05 is expected, but a candidate who works with expected payoffs 118 and 114 and discounts at the end loses nothing.",
        "Builds the state-by-state table with debt = min(CF, K) and equity = max(CF − K, 0), and puts the zero in R's bust state: equity is 0, not −60. Notes that the debt is risk free under S (D_S = 85.7143) and risky under R.",
        "Compares the EQUITY values, 45.7143 against 26.6667, to determine the choice, and concludes that management runs R. The error the keys single out is comparing the strategies' stand-alone NPVs instead of the change in equity value: what is efficient and what management chooses are different questions here, and that gap is the whole exercise.",
        "Names the mechanism: risk shifting or asset substitution, with equity a convex call option on firm value struck at the face value K.",
        "Reports the agency cost as first-best value minus chosen value, 112.3810 − 108.5714 = 3.8095, and not as the 22.8571 transferred away from creditors nor the 19.0476 gained by shareholders.",
        "Runs consistency check 3 explicitly with both signs: −22.8571 + 19.0476 = −3.8095 = the NPV of the switch. Every figure shown: no calculation, no points."
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "k9-2",
      open: true,
      topic: "The indifference face value",
      points: 8,
      body: "<p>Rørvik Kjemi AS will be run as one of two mutually exclusive strategies for one year. The two states are equally likely, investors are risk neutral, and r<sub>f</sub> = 0, so every value is simply an expected payoff. Neither strategy requires new investment. Figures in millions of NOK.</p><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>State</th><th>Probability</th><th>Cash flow under S</th><th>Cash flow under R</th></tr><tr><td>Up</td><td class=\"n\">0.50</td><td class=\"n\">150</td><td class=\"n\">220</td></tr><tr><td>Down</td><td class=\"n\">0.50</td><td class=\"n\">110</td><td class=\"n\">12</td></tr></table><p>The firm has zero-coupon debt outstanding with face value K, and management acts for the shareholders.</p><p>(a) For which face values K is the debt risky under S, and for which is it risky under R? (2 points)</p><p>(b) Write equity value as a function of K under each strategy on the interval where the same states default, state that interval explicitly, and solve for the face value K* at which management is indifferent between the two strategies. Verify your solution. (4 points)</p><p>(c) Tabulate E<sub>S</sub> and E<sub>R</sub> at K = 0, 12, 40, 100 and 130, state what management does in each row, and explain why no second indifference point exists outside your interval. (2 points)</p>",
      solution: "<p><b>Setup.</b> With r<sub>f</sub> = 0, V<sub>S</sub> = 0.50 × 150 + 0.50 × 110 = <b>130</b> and V<sub>R</sub> = 0.50 × 220 + 0.50 × 12 = <b>116</b>, so R destroys 130 − 116 = 14 of firm value whatever K turns out to be. The whole question is at which K the shareholders stop caring about that.</p><p><b>(a) When the debt is risky.</b> Debt is risky as soon as the promise K exceeds the cash flow in the worst state, because then creditors take a loss in that state.<br>Under S the worst state pays 110, so the debt is risk free for K ≤ 110 and <b>risky for K &gt; 110</b>.<br>Under R the worst state pays 12, so the debt is risk free for K ≤ 12 and <b>risky for K &gt; 12</b>.<br>That already tells you where the conflict can live: only where at least one strategy defaults, that is K &gt; 12.</p><p><b>(b) Equity as a function of K.</b> Choose the interval <b>12 &lt; K ≤ 110</b>. On it, S pays the debt in full in both states while R defaults in the down state, so the same states default throughout and both equity functions are linear. The max(·, 0) is what makes the functions piecewise linear, so the algebra is valid only inside one piece: state the interval or the answer is not defended.</p><p>E<sub>S</sub>(K) = 0.50(150 − K) + 0.50(110 − K) = 75 − 0.5K + 55 − 0.5K = <b>130 − K</b><br>E<sub>R</sub>(K) = 0.50(220 − K) + 0.50 × 0 = <b>110 − 0.5K</b></p><p>Set them equal:<br>130 − K = 110 − 0.5K<br>130 − 110 = K − 0.5K<br>20 = 0.5K<br><b>K* = 40</b></p><p><b>Verification:</b> E<sub>S</sub>(40) = 130 − 40 = 90 and E<sub>R</sub>(40) = 110 − 0.5 × 40 = 110 − 20 = 90 ✓ Both equal 90, and 40 lies inside the stated interval (12, 110], so the solution is admissible. Cross-check the split at K = 40: under S the debt is risk free at D = 40 and 40 + 90 = 130 = V<sub>S</sub> ✓, while under R the debt is worth 0.50 × 40 + 0.50 × 12 = 26 and 26 + 90 = 116 = V<sub>R</sub> ✓ Same equity value, 14 less for the creditors: that is exactly the value R destroys.</p><p><b>Reading K*.</b> Below K = 40 shareholders prefer the safe strategy, above it they gamble. Note that the debt turns risky at K = 12, well before the incentive flips at 40: risky debt is necessary for risk shifting but not sufficient. The gamble is taken only once the transfer it extracts from creditors exceeds the 14 it destroys, and that takes a face value of 40.</p><p><b>(c) The table.</b></p><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Face value K</th><th>E<sub>S</sub></th><th>E<sub>R</sub></th><th>Management runs</th></tr><tr><td class=\"n\">0</td><td class=\"n\">130</td><td class=\"n\">116</td><td>S</td></tr><tr><td class=\"n\">12</td><td class=\"n\">118</td><td class=\"n\">104</td><td>S</td></tr><tr><td class=\"n\">40</td><td class=\"n\">90</td><td class=\"n\">90</td><td>indifferent</td></tr><tr><td class=\"n\">100</td><td class=\"n\">30</td><td class=\"n\">60</td><td>R</td></tr><tr><td class=\"n\">130</td><td class=\"n\">10</td><td class=\"n\">45</td><td>R</td></tr></table><p>The K = 130 row uses the next piece, since 130 &gt; 110: E<sub>S</sub>(130) = 0.50(150 − 130) = 10 and E<sub>R</sub>(130) = 0.50(220 − 130) = 45.</p><p><b>Why there is no second crossing.</b> Check the other two pieces by writing their equations out.<br>For 0 ≤ K ≤ 12 neither strategy defaults, so E<sub>S</sub> = 130 − K and E<sub>R</sub> = 116 − K. The gap is a constant 14 in favour of S, exactly the value R destroys: with risk-free debt equity is linear in the cash flow, expectations pass straight through, and the shareholder is a residual claimant on the whole firm, so he ranks the strategies the way the firm does.<br>For 110 &lt; K ≤ 150 both strategies default in the down state, so E<sub>S</sub> = 0.50(150 − K) and E<sub>R</sub> = 0.50(220 − K). The gap is a constant 35 in favour of R. Neither equation has a root, so K* = 40 is unique, and for every K above it management gambles.</p>",
      criteria: [
        "Identifies the two risky-debt thresholds from the worst state of each strategy: K > 110 under S and K > 12 under R, and notes that K > 12 is where a conflict becomes possible at all.",
        "States the interval 12 < K ≤ 110 explicitly before doing the algebra, and says why it is needed: max(CF − K, 0) makes both equity functions piecewise linear, so a linear equation is only valid inside one piece.",
        "Derives E_S(K) = 130 − K and E_R(K) = 110 − 0.5K, solves 130 − K = 110 − 0.5K to K* = 40, and verifies that both sides equal 90 and that 40 lies inside the stated interval.",
        "Tabulates the choice correctly, including the K = 130 row, which needs the next piece because S now defaults too: E_S = 10 and E_R = 45.",
        "Shows that no second indifference point exists, by writing the equations on the other two pieces and observing the constant gaps of 14 (S preferred) and 35 (R preferred).",
        "Distinguishes the two thresholds and does not confuse them: the debt becomes risky at K = 12, but the shareholders' preference flips only at K = 40, once the transfer from creditors outweighs the 14 that R destroys. Comparing the strategies' stand-alone values instead of the equity values gives S at every K and misses the question."
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "k9-3",
      open: true,
      topic: "Will shareholders accept a zero-NPV hedge?",
      points: 8,
      body: "<p>Sunnhordland Aluminium AS will generate a single cash flow in one year: 160 if the metal price is high and 40 if it is low, the two states being equally likely. The firm has zero-coupon debt outstanding with face value K = 90, maturing at the same date. Investors are risk neutral and r<sub>f</sub> = 0. Figures in millions of NOK.</p><p>A bank offers a costless forward contract on the firm's output. Entering it converts the firm's cash flow into a certain 100 in both states: the firm pays 60 away in the high state and receives 60 in the low state. The contract has a price of zero today and no counterparty is paid a fee.</p><p>(a) Confirm that the hedge is a zero-NPV transaction for the firm, then value debt and equity state by state with and without it. Does management, acting for the shareholders, enter the contract? (3 points)</p><p>(b) Run the consistency check that applies when total firm value is fixed, and say who gains what. Show that consistency check 3 also holds here, and explain why it is uninformative in this case. (2 points)</p><p>(c) For which face values K would the shareholders be willing to hedge? Derive the answer, and name the mechanism that explains the pattern. (3 points)</p>",
      solution: "<p><b>(a) The hedge is zero-NPV, and it still costs the shareholders money.</b> Expected cash flow unhedged is 0.50 × 160 + 0.50 × 40 = <b>100</b>; hedged it is 100 with certainty, so the expected payoff is <b>unchanged at 100</b> and, with r<sub>f</sub> = 0 and risk-neutral investors, firm value is 100 either way. The hedge shifts 60 from the high state to the low state and creates nothing: <b>NPV = 0</b> ✓</p><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>State (prob.)</th><th>CF unhedged</th><th>Debt</th><th>Equity</th><th>CF hedged</th><th>Debt</th><th>Equity</th></tr><tr><td>High (0.50)</td><td class=\"n\">160</td><td class=\"n\">90</td><td class=\"n\">70</td><td class=\"n\">100</td><td class=\"n\">90</td><td class=\"n\">10</td></tr><tr><td>Low (0.50)</td><td class=\"n\">40</td><td class=\"n\">40</td><td class=\"n\">0</td><td class=\"n\">100</td><td class=\"n\">90</td><td class=\"n\">10</td></tr><tr><td><b>Value</b></td><td class=\"n\"><b>100</b></td><td class=\"n\"><b>65</b></td><td class=\"n\"><b>35</b></td><td class=\"n\"><b>100</b></td><td class=\"n\"><b>90</b></td><td class=\"n\"><b>10</b></td></tr></table><p>Unhedged: D = 0.50 × 90 + 0.50 × 40 = 45 + 20 = <b>65</b> and E = 0.50 × 70 + 0.50 × 0 = <b>35</b>; check D + E = 65 + 35 = 100 ✓ The debt is <b>risky</b>, promised 90 but worth 65, because the low state pays creditors only 40.<br>Hedged: the certain 100 covers the promise of 90 in both states, so the debt becomes <b>risk free</b> at D = <b>90</b>, and E = 100 − 90 = <b>10</b>; check 90 + 10 = 100 ✓</p><p><b>The decision: management does not hedge.</b> Equity falls from 35 to 10, a loss of 25, so the shareholders reject a contract that is free to the firm. The refusal is not irrational risk-seeking, it is arithmetic on their own claim.</p><p><b>(b) Consistency check 4 (creditors' gain = shareholders' loss when total value is fixed).</b> Total value is 100 with and without the hedge, so nothing is created or destroyed and the hedge is a <b>pure transfer</b>:<br>creditors' gain = 90 − 65 = <b>+25</b><br>shareholders' loss = 35 − 10 = <b>25</b><br>+25 = 25 ✓ The whole 25 that the shareholders give up lands with the creditors, who are the ones who want this hedge.</p><p><b>Consistency check 3 in the same numbers:</b> creditors' gain + shareholders' gain = +25 + (−25) = 0 = the NPV of the hedge ✓ It holds, but it holds <b>weakly</b>: the hedge is zero-NPV by construction, so check 3 only asks whether the two changes cancel, which is the same statement as check 4 here. Say so rather than presenting it as independent evidence. Check 4 is the sharper one, because it names what is happening: transfer, not destruction. The agency cost of a refused zero-NPV hedge is zero in this model; the loss shows up only when the hedge itself would have created value, for example by avoiding distress costs, or when the refusal locks in a value-destroying strategy.</p><p><b>(c) At which face values would shareholders hedge?</b> Write both equity values as functions of K and compare. Unhedged, the low state pays 40 and the high state 160; hedged, both pay 100.<br>E<sup>unhedged</sup>(K) = 0.50 max(160 − K, 0) + 0.50 max(40 − K, 0)<br>E<sup>hedged</sup>(K) = max(100 − K, 0)</p><p><i>For 0 ≤ K ≤ 40:</i> nothing defaults either way, so E<sup>unhedged</sup> = 0.50(160 − K) + 0.50(40 − K) = 100 − K and E<sup>hedged</sup> = 100 − K. <b>They are equal at every K in this range: the shareholders are indifferent.</b><br><i>For 40 &lt; K ≤ 100:</i> the unhedged firm defaults in the low state, so E<sup>unhedged</sup> = 0.50(160 − K) = 80 − 0.5K, while E<sup>hedged</sup> = 100 − K. The difference is (80 − 0.5K) − (100 − K) = 0.5K − 20, which is strictly positive for K &gt; 40, so <b>the shareholders strictly refuse</b>. At K = 90 the gap is 0.5 × 90 − 20 = 25, the loss computed in (a) ✓<br><i>For 100 &lt; K ≤ 160:</i> E<sup>hedged</sup> = 0 while E<sup>unhedged</sup> = 0.50(160 − K) &gt; 0, so they refuse even more emphatically.<br><b>Answer: shareholders accept the hedge only for K ≤ 40, where they are indifferent, and strictly refuse for every K &gt; 40.</b> (For K &gt; 160 both claims are worthless and the comparison is degenerate.) The threshold 40 is exactly the point at which the debt becomes risky.</p><p><b>Name the mechanism.</b> A hedge is neutral for shareholders <b>if and only if the debt is risk free</b>. With safe debt the equity payoff is the linear CF − K, the max(·, 0) never binds, and an expectation-preserving reshuffle of the states passes straight through and leaves equity untouched. Once the debt is risky the equity claim is a <b>call option on firm value struck at K</b>, and a call is convex, so its value depends on the whole distribution and not just its mean: removing the spread removes the option value. This is the mirror image of risk shifting. Shareholders take a value-destroying gamble for the same reason they refuse a free hedge, namely that the downside beyond K is not theirs.</p><p><b>Can the parties fix it?</b> Not on their own here. To make the shareholders willing to hedge, the face value would have to be cut to K′ with 100 − K′ = 35, that is K′ = <b>65</b>, and the creditors would then hold a risk-free claim worth 65, exactly the 65 they hold today. There is no surplus to divide, because the hedge creates nothing: it is a distribution question, and the answer is a covenant written before the fact. Had the hedging policy been in the bond indenture at issue, the creditors would have paid 90 rather than 65 for the bond and the shareholders would have kept the 25 as cash raised.</p>",
      criteria: [
        "Confirms the hedge is zero-NPV before valuing anything: expected cash flow is 100 with and without it, so firm value is 100 either way and nothing is created.",
        "Values the claims state by state: unhedged D = 65 and E = 35 with risky debt, hedged D = 90 and E = 10 with risk-free debt, each pair summing to 100. Concludes that management refuses the hedge because equity falls from 35 to 10.",
        "Runs consistency check 4 by name, creditors' gain +25 equals shareholders' loss 25 with total value fixed, and labels the hedge a transfer rather than a deadweight loss. Says honestly that check 3 adds nothing here because the hedge is zero-NPV by construction.",
        "Derives the acceptance region rather than asserting it: equity is 100 − K both ways for K ≤ 40, and the unhedged claim 80 − 0.5K exceeds the hedged 100 − K by 0.5K − 20 for 40 < K ≤ 100. Answer: indifferent for K ≤ 40, strictly refuses above, and 40 is where the debt turns risky.",
        "Names the mechanism: equity is a convex call on firm value struck at K, so with risky debt it depends on the whole distribution and not only its mean; a hedge is neutral only when the debt is risk free.",
        "Does not confuse the transaction's stand-alone NPV with the change in equity value. The hedge has NPV = 0 and is still rejected: the classic wrong answer is that shareholders should be indifferent because the NPV is zero. H2024 Exercise 4 and V2024 Problem 1 both end on exactly this sub-question."
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "k9-4",
      open: true,
      topic: "Who bears the agency cost",
      points: 8,
      body: "<p>Telemark Prosess AS has zero-coupon debt outstanding with face value K = 90, maturing in one year, and no other liabilities. Management can run the assets under strategy A or under strategy B, and neither requires new investment. Cash flow arrives in one year in one of three states. Investors are risk neutral and r<sub>f</sub> = 0. Figures in millions of NOK.</p><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>State</th><th>Probability</th><th>Cash flow under A</th><th>Cash flow under B</th></tr><tr><td>Good</td><td class=\"n\">0.25</td><td class=\"n\">120</td><td class=\"n\">200</td></tr><tr><td>Middle</td><td class=\"n\">0.50</td><td class=\"n\">100</td><td class=\"n\">90</td></tr><tr><td>Bad</td><td class=\"n\">0.25</td><td class=\"n\">80</td><td class=\"n\">0</td></tr></table><p>(a) Value debt and equity state by state under each strategy. Which strategy does management run, and what is the agency cost of debt? (3 points)</p><p>(b) Run consistency check 3 on the switch. (1 point)</p><p>(c) The bond has not yet been issued. Competitive creditors correctly anticipate which strategy will be run and price the bond accordingly. What do they pay for a bond with face value 90, and what is the original owners' total wealth? Compare with a world in which the owners could commit to A, and state who bears the agency cost. (2 points)</p><p>(d) With the bond already outstanding, the creditors offer to cut the face value from 90 to K′ in exchange for strategy A. Find the range of K′ that leaves both sides at least as well off, then explain in two sentences why this agreement is not self-enforcing. (2 points)</p>",
      solution: "<p><b>Setup.</b> With r<sub>f</sub> = 0 every value is an expected payoff. Firm values first:<br>V<sub>A</sub> = 0.25 × 120 + 0.50 × 100 + 0.25 × 80 = 30 + 50 + 20 = <b>100</b><br>V<sub>B</sub> = 0.25 × 200 + 0.50 × 90 + 0.25 × 0 = 50 + 45 + 0 = <b>95</b><br>A is the first best, and switching to B has NPV = 95 − 100 = <b>−5</b>.</p><p><b>(a) The split.</b> Creditors take min(CF, 90), shareholders max(CF − 90, 0). Note the two zeros: equity is 0 in A's bad state (80 &lt; 90) and 0 in both B's middle and bad states.</p><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>State (prob.)</th><th>CF under A</th><th>Debt</th><th>Equity</th><th>CF under B</th><th>Debt</th><th>Equity</th></tr><tr><td>Good (0.25)</td><td class=\"n\">120</td><td class=\"n\">90</td><td class=\"n\">30</td><td class=\"n\">200</td><td class=\"n\">90</td><td class=\"n\">110</td></tr><tr><td>Middle (0.50)</td><td class=\"n\">100</td><td class=\"n\">90</td><td class=\"n\">10</td><td class=\"n\">90</td><td class=\"n\">90</td><td class=\"n\">0</td></tr><tr><td>Bad (0.25)</td><td class=\"n\">80</td><td class=\"n\">80</td><td class=\"n\">0</td><td class=\"n\">0</td><td class=\"n\">0</td><td class=\"n\">0</td></tr><tr><td><b>Value</b></td><td class=\"n\"><b>100</b></td><td class=\"n\"><b>87.5</b></td><td class=\"n\"><b>12.5</b></td><td class=\"n\"><b>95</b></td><td class=\"n\"><b>67.5</b></td><td class=\"n\"><b>27.5</b></td></tr></table><p>Arithmetic: D<sub>A</sub> = 0.25 × 90 + 0.50 × 90 + 0.25 × 80 = 22.5 + 45 + 20 = 87.5 and E<sub>A</sub> = 0.25 × 30 + 0.50 × 10 = 7.5 + 5 = 12.5; check 87.5 + 12.5 = 100 ✓ Then D<sub>B</sub> = 22.5 + 45 + 0 = 67.5 and E<sub>B</sub> = 0.25 × 110 = 27.5; check 67.5 + 27.5 = 95 ✓</p><p><b>The choice.</b> E<sub>B</sub> = 27.5 exceeds E<sub>A</sub> = 12.5, so <b>management runs B</b> even though B is worth 5 less. This is <b>risk shifting (asset substitution)</b>: B adds 80 in the good state, which the shareholders keep in full, and removes 10 in the middle state and 80 in the bad state, both of which fall on creditors who are already short there.</p><p><b>agency cost = V(first best) − V(chosen) = 100 − 95 = 5</b></p><p><b>(b) Consistency check 3.</b><br>creditors' gain = D<sub>B</sub> − D<sub>A</sub> = 67.5 − 87.5 = <b>−20</b><br>shareholders' gain = E<sub>B</sub> − E<sub>A</sub> = 27.5 − 12.5 = <b>+15</b><br>Sum: −20 + 15 = −5 = the NPV of switching ✓ The creditors lose 20, of which 15 is transferred to the shareholders and 5 evaporates. Only the 5 is the agency cost.</p><p><b>(c) Pricing the bond at issue.</b> Competitive creditors break even, so they pay what the claim is actually worth given the strategy they expect. They expect B, so they pay <b>67.5</b> for a bond promising 90, not 87.5 and certainly not 90. The original owners then hold the cash raised plus the equity:<br>67.5 + 27.5 = <b>95</b><br>If instead they could commit at issue to running A, the bond would sell for 87.5 and their wealth would be 87.5 + 12.5 = <b>100</b>.<br><b>The original owners bear the agency cost</b>, 100 − 95 = 5, in the form of a lower issue price. This is consistency check 5 in action: the creditors' beliefs are rational given the action that will be taken, so the transfer the shareholders were counting on has already been priced away, and only the deadweight loss is left for them to carry. The 15 they appeared to gain in (b) is a gain relative to creditors who were naive, and no such creditors exist in equilibrium.</p><p><b>(d) The renegotiation range.</b> Both sides must be at least as well off as under the status quo, where the creditors hold 67.5 and the shareholders 27.5. Under A with a new face value K′ ≤ 80 the debt is risk free, because A's worst state pays 80, so D = K′ and E = 100 − K′.<br>Creditors: K′ ≥ 67.5<br>Shareholders: 100 − K′ ≥ 27.5 ⟹ K′ ≤ 72.5<br><b>K′ ∈ [67.5, 72.5]</b> Any K′ in this interval works, and the whole surplus being divided is the agency cost of 5. At the midpoint K′ = 70 the creditors hold 70, up 2.5, the shareholders hold 30, up 2.5, and 70 + 30 = 100 = V<sub>A</sub> ✓ The 2.5 + 2.5 = 5 they split is exactly the value that risk shifting was destroying.</p><p><b>Why it is not self-enforcing.</b> Once the face value has been cut to 70 the shareholders' incentive has not gone away: on the interval K ≤ 80 the equity values are E<sub>A</sub>(K) = 100 − K and E<sub>B</sub>(K) = 0.25(200 − K) + 0.50(90 − K) = 95 − 0.75K, which cross at <b>K* = 20</b>, so at K′ = 70 the shareholders would still prefer B, worth 95 − 52.5 = 42.5 against 30 for A, and they would take it the moment the ink dried. Self-enforcement would require cutting the face value below 20, at which point the creditors would hold at most 20 against the 67.5 they have now and would never agree. There is therefore no debt reduction that is both acceptable to the creditors and incentive compatible: the deal only works if strategy A is written into the indenture as an enforceable covenant.</p>",
      criteria: [
        "Builds the three-state table with debt = min(CF, 90) and equity = max(CF − 90, 0), getting D_A = 87.5, E_A = 12.5, D_B = 67.5, E_B = 27.5, each pair summing to the firm value 100 and 95. The zeros in B's middle and bad states must be there.",
        "Chooses B on the equity comparison 27.5 > 12.5, names risk shifting or asset substitution, and reports the agency cost as 100 − 95 = 5. Reporting the 20 the creditors lose, or the 15 the shareholders gain, as the agency cost is the error the keys name; so is ranking the strategies by their stand-alone values, which answers a question that was not asked.",
        "Runs consistency check 3 with both signs: −20 + 15 = −5 = the NPV of the switch.",
        "Prices the bond at 67.5 at issue, not 87.5 or 90, because competitive creditors anticipate B, and concludes that the original owners' wealth is 67.5 + 27.5 = 95 against 100 with commitment, so they bear the agency cost of 5 through the issue price.",
        "Derives the renegotiation range from two participation constraints, K′ ≥ 67.5 for creditors and K′ ≤ 72.5 for shareholders, and notes that the surplus being split is exactly the agency cost of 5.",
        "Sees that the renegotiated contract is not incentive compatible: the indifference face value is K* = 20, so at any K′ in the acceptable range the shareholders would still run B, and an enforceable covenant rather than a lower face value is what is needed."
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "k9-5",
      open: true,
      topic: "Contractual remedies for risk shifting",
      points: 6,
      body: "<p>Vikna Offshore AS is about to issue five-year zero-coupon debt. Its assets are easy to redeploy into far riskier ventures at short notice, its equity is deep out of the money, and prospective creditors say so openly: they will not lend at anything the owners consider a reasonable price. Three remedies are on the table.</p><p>For <b>each</b> remedy, say through which mechanism it reduces risk shifting, name the mechanism, state whether it creates value or merely transfers it and who ends up better off, and give one cost or limitation. <b>2–3 sentences per remedy.</b> Length is not rewarded; the named mechanism is. (2 points each)</p><p>(a) A negative covenant in the indenture restricting asset sales, restricting further borrowing, capping dividends and share repurchases, and requiring the firm's commodity exposure to be hedged.</p><p>(b) Issuing one-year debt rolled over annually instead of five-year debt.</p><p>(c) Issuing convertible debt, giving the creditors the right to convert into a fixed fraction of the equity at maturity, in place of straight debt.</p>",
      solution: "<p><b>The frame to put first.</b> Risk shifting is a problem because equity is a call option on firm value struck at the face value K, so shareholders gain from volatility that the firm as a whole loses on. Every remedy below works in one of two ways: it <b>removes the option</b> to raise risk after the money is in, or it <b>removes the convexity</b> by giving creditors a share of the upside. And in every case the gain is captured by the shareholders ex ante, through a higher price for the bond, which is why the owners of a firm like Vikna volunteer for restrictions rather than resist them.</p><p><b>(a) Covenants: ex-ante commitment.</b> A negative covenant works by contracting away the very action that would shift risk, so the promise the shareholders cannot make credibly with words is made enforceable by the indenture and by the lender's right to accelerate the loan if it is broken; name it a <b>bonding or commitment device</b>, and note that the hedging clause is the direct answer to the shareholders who refuse a free hedge once the debt is risky. It <b>creates value</b>, because the agency cost is a deadweight loss and avoiding it makes the pie larger, and the shareholders capture that gain at issue: creditors who believe the covenant pay the risk-free price for the bond, so the owners raise more today. The cost is lost flexibility and the risk of over-restriction: a covenant tight enough to block the gamble can also block a genuinely good project that happens to raise volatility, and covenants must be monitored, renegotiated and sometimes waived, all of which is expensive.</p><p><b>(b) Short maturity: repricing and the right to walk away.</b> Rolling the debt annually works by moving the creditors' pricing decision to just after each year's observation of the firm's risk, so an increase in risk is paid for by the shareholders in next year's rollover rate rather than expropriated from a locked-in lender; name it <b>repricing, or maturity as a monitoring device</b>, reinforced by the fact that short-dated debt shortens the shareholders' option and cuts its time value, which is where the gain from volatility lives. It <b>creates value</b> by shrinking the window in which risk can be shifted without consequence, and again the shareholders capture it in the lower yield they are quoted at issue. The cost is <b>rollover and liquidity risk</b>: a solvent firm can be forced into default because a refinancing falls due in a bad market, so short maturity trades an agency cost for a distress risk, and the trade is a bad one for a firm whose assets are long-lived.</p><p><b>(c) Convertible debt: removing the convexity.</b> A conversion right gives the creditor the upper tail that the shareholders were gambling for, so the creditor's claim becomes convex too and the shareholders no longer keep the whole of the gain from added volatility; name the mechanism <b>giving the creditor an equity stake to align the payoffs</b>, and note that it is the cleanest remedy in principle because it changes the incentive itself rather than policing the action. It <b>creates value</b> by removing the distortion, and the shareholders are paid for the conversion option in the issue price. The cost is <b>dilution</b>, since the shareholders sell away the upside of the good outcomes too, and the alignment is imperfect: the conversion feature blunts risk shifting rather than eliminating it, and it does nothing about the other agency cost of debt, debt overhang, where the same creditors' improved claim is precisely what deters investment.</p><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Remedy</th><th>Mechanism, by name</th><th>What it costs</th></tr><tr><td>Negative covenants</td><td>Commitment: the action is contracted away and the lender can accelerate if it is broken</td><td>Lost flexibility, monitoring and waiver costs, and good projects blocked with the bad</td></tr><tr><td>Short maturity, rollover</td><td>Repricing: risk taken this year is paid for in next year's rate, and the shareholders' option is shorter and worth less</td><td>Rollover and liquidity risk; a solvent firm can default at a refinancing</td></tr><tr><td>Convertible debt</td><td>The creditor's claim is made convex too, so the upper tail is no longer the shareholders' alone</td><td>Dilution; blunts rather than removes the distortion, and does nothing for debt overhang</td></tr><tr><td>Collateral, secured debt</td><td>The loan is tied to a named asset that cannot be substituted</td><td>Only works for assets that are identifiable and hard to move</td></tr><tr><td>Less leverage at issue</td><td>Moves the face value below the indifference level K*, so the incentive never arises</td><td>Gives up the interest tax shield and the agency benefits of debt</td></tr></table><p><b>Two more that would score.</b> A sinking fund or an amortising loan, which shortens effective maturity without the full rollover risk, and bank rather than bond finance, where a concentrated lender monitors continuously and can renegotiate instead of waiting for default. The one thing that never works is a verbal promise: the shareholders have an incentive to break it, the creditors know that, and it is therefore neither believed nor priced.</p>",
      criteria: [
        "Names a mechanism for each remedy rather than describing it: commitment or bonding for the covenant, repricing at rollover (and a shorter, less valuable option) for short maturity, and giving the creditor a convex claim or an equity stake for the convertible.",
        "Says for each remedy that value is created, not merely transferred, because the agency cost is a deadweight loss, and identifies the shareholders as the ones who end up better off through the higher price the bond fetches at issue.",
        "Gives one genuine cost per remedy: lost flexibility and monitoring or waiver costs for covenants, rollover and liquidity risk for short maturity, dilution and imperfect alignment for the convertible.",
        "Grounds at least one answer in the mechanics of the chapter, for example that a hedging covenant is the answer to shareholders refusing a zero-NPV hedge, or that lower leverage works by pushing the face value below the indifference level K* at which the incentive flips.",
        "Keeps to 2–3 sentences per remedy, as instructed. Bonus for one extra well-argued point, such as naming collateral or a sinking fund, or noting that the convertible does nothing for debt overhang.",
        "Does not claim the remedies benefit creditors at the shareholders' expense. Ex ante the creditors break even whatever the contract says, and the whole efficiency gain accrues to the owners: an answer that has shareholders grudgingly accepting restrictions imposed on them has the incentives backwards."
      ]
    }
  ]
};

/* Chapter 12 — Asymmetric information: Myers-Majluf */
