window.EDU_DATA.chapterTasks[22] = {
  minutes: 72,
  tasks: [
    {
      id: "k22-1",
      open: true,
      topic: "Replication",
      points: 8,
      body: "<p>Fjordvang Industri ASA trades at S<sub>0</sub> = 250.00 (all figures in NOK). In one year the share will be worth either S<sub>u</sub> = 325.00 or S<sub>d</sub> = 200.00, and nothing else can happen. The one-year risk-free rate is r<sub>f</sub> = 6%. Consider a one-year European call on the share with strike K = 270.</p><p>(a) Show first that the tree admits no arbitrage on its own. Then build the replicating portfolio: compute Δ and B, price the call as C = S<sub>0</sub>Δ + B, and verify state by state that the portfolio pays exactly what the call pays. (3 points)</p><p>(b) Price the same call with the risk-neutral probability ρ. Check that ρ prices the share itself, and say in one or two sentences why the two routes cannot give different answers. (3 points)</p><p>(c) A broker quotes the call at 28.50. Construct the arbitrage: state the position, the net cash today, and the payoff in both states in one year. Verify that the profit equals the mispricing. (2 points)</p>",
      solution: "<p><b>(a) Replication.</b> The bound first, because it costs one line and an inconsistent tree makes everything below meaningless: S<sub>d</sub> &lt; (1 + r<sub>f</sub>)S<sub>0</sub> &lt; S<sub>u</sub>, that is 200.00 &lt; 1.06 × 250.00 = 265.00 &lt; 325.00 ✓ The share beats the bond in one state and loses to it in the other, so neither dominates.</p><p>Payoffs: C<sub>u</sub> = max(325.00 − 270, 0) = <b>55.00</b> and C<sub>d</sub> = max(200.00 − 270, 0) = <b>0.00</b>.</p><p>Δ = (C<sub>u</sub> − C<sub>d</sub>)/(S<sub>u</sub> − S<sub>d</sub>) = (55.00 − 0.00)/(325.00 − 200.00) = 55.00/125.00 = <b>0.4400</b><br>B = (C<sub>d</sub> − S<sub>d</sub>Δ)/(1 + r<sub>f</sub>) = (0.00 − 200.00 × 0.4400)/1.06 = −88.00/1.06 = <b>−83.0189</b><br>C = S<sub>0</sub>Δ + B = 250.00 × 0.4400 − 83.0189 = 110.0000 − 83.0189 = <b>26.9811</b></p><p>B is negative, so you borrow 83.0189 today and repay 83.0189 × 1.06 = 88.00 in one year. Verify the portfolio in both states, which is the step that earns the marks:</p><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>State</th><th>0.44 shares are worth</th><th>Loan repayment</th><th>Portfolio</th><th>Call payoff</th></tr><tr><td>Up, S<sub>u</sub> = 325.00</td><td class=\"n\">143.00</td><td class=\"n\">−88.00</td><td class=\"n\">55.00</td><td class=\"n\">55.00</td></tr><tr><td>Down, S<sub>d</sub> = 200.00</td><td class=\"n\">88.00</td><td class=\"n\">−88.00</td><td class=\"n\">0.00</td><td class=\"n\">0.00</td></tr></table><p>The match is exact in both states, so the call is that package and must cost what the package costs: <b>26.9811</b>.</p><p><b>(b) The risk-neutral route.</b></p><p>ρ = [(1 + r<sub>f</sub>)S<sub>0</sub> − S<sub>d</sub>]/(S<sub>u</sub> − S<sub>d</sub>) = (265.00 − 200.00)/125.00 = 65.00/125.00 = <b>0.5200</b>, and 1 − ρ = 0.4800.</p><p>Check that ρ prices the share before using it on anything else: [0.5200 × 325.00 + 0.4800 × 200.00]/1.06 = (169.0000 + 96.0000)/1.06 = 265.0000/1.06 = <b>250.00</b> = S<sub>0</sub> ✓ One line, and it catches a transposed S<sub>u</sub> and S<sub>d</sub>.</p><p>C<sub>0</sub> = [ρC<sub>u</sub> + (1 − ρ)C<sub>d</sub>]/(1 + r<sub>f</sub>) = [0.5200 × 55.00 + 0.4800 × 0.00]/1.06 = 28.6000/1.06 = <b>26.9811</b> ✓ Identical to (a).</p><p><b>Why they cannot differ.</b> The second route is the first one rearranged, not a second opinion: substitute B = (C<sub>d</sub> − S<sub>d</sub>Δ)/(1 + r<sub>f</sub>) into C = S<sub>0</sub>Δ + B, collect terms in C<sub>u</sub> and C<sub>d</sub>, and the weights that appear are ρ and 1 − ρ. Both are therefore the same no-arbitrage price, and agreement between them is an identity that locates arithmetic slips rather than an independent test of the answer. Note the one thing that has to be right in the numerator of ρ: S<sub>0</sub> must be grown to 265.00 first. Using 250.00 gives (250.00 − 200.00)/125.00 = 0.4000 and a call of 0.4000 × 55.00/1.06 = 20.7547, which is the certainty-equivalent error the solution keys single out.</p><p><b>(c) The arbitrage.</b> The quote 28.50 exceeds the replicating cost 26.9811, so the call is dear: <b>sell the call and buy the portfolio</b>. A quick direction test before you write anything: the quoted price is too high, so you must be on the selling side of the option.</p><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Leg</th><th>Action today</th><th>Cash today</th><th>Up, 325.00</th><th>Down, 200.00</th></tr><tr><td>Option</td><td>Write one call at 28.50</td><td class=\"n\">+28.50</td><td class=\"n\">−55.00</td><td class=\"n\">0.00</td></tr><tr><td>Shares</td><td>Buy 0.44 shares at 250.00</td><td class=\"n\">−110.0000</td><td class=\"n\">+143.00</td><td class=\"n\">+88.00</td></tr><tr><td>Bond</td><td>Borrow 83.0189 at 6%</td><td class=\"n\">+83.0189</td><td class=\"n\">−88.00</td><td class=\"n\">−88.00</td></tr><tr><td><b>Net</b></td><td></td><td class=\"n\"><b>+1.5189</b></td><td class=\"n\"><b>0.00</b></td><td class=\"n\"><b>0.00</b></td></tr></table><p>You collect 1.5189 today and owe nothing in either state in one year, which is the definition of an arbitrage.</p><p><b>Check:</b> the profit must equal the mispricing, 28.5000 − 26.9811 = <b>1.5189</b> ✓ If your profit does not reproduce the mispricing you have taken the wrong side, or your Δ and B do not belong to the same tree. Note that the trade works without any view on the share: the two future columns are zero whatever happens, so you never need to know how likely the up state is.</p>",
      criteria: [
        "Checks the no-arbitrage bound 200.00 below 265.00 below 325.00 before computing anything, stating that 265.00 is S_0 grown at r_f.",
        "Δ = 55/125 = 0.4400 as a ratio of DIFFERENCES, not of levels: Δ = C_u/S_u = 0.1692 is a standard slip. B = −88.00/1.06 = −83.0189, discounted; writing B = −88.00 skips the discounting and gives 22.0000 instead of 26.9811.",
        "Shows the state-by-state table: 143.00 − 88.00 = 55.00 and 88.00 − 88.00 = 0.00. Without it the replication is asserted rather than demonstrated, and no calculation means no points.",
        "ρ = (1.06 × 250 − 200)/125 = 0.5200, with S_0 grown at the risk-free rate BEFORE S_d is subtracted. Using 250 instead of 265 gives 0.4000 and a call of 20.7547, which is the certainty-equivalent error the keys call out. Runs the one-line check that ρ reprices the share at 250.00.",
        "Both routes give 26.9811 and the answer says why they must: the ρ formula is the replication equation rearranged, so the agreement is an identity that catches arithmetic, not a second independent estimate.",
        "Gets the arbitrage direction right, sell the dear call and buy the portfolio, shows net cash +1.5189 today and zero in both future states, and checks that 1.5189 equals the mispricing 28.5000 − 26.9811."
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "k22-2",
      open: true,
      topic: "Real probabilities",
      points: 8,
      body: "<p>Nordlys Batteri ASA trades at S<sub>0</sub> = 160.00 (all figures in NOK). In one year the share will be worth S<sub>u</sub> = 240.00 or S<sub>d</sub> = 120.00. The one-year risk-free rate is r<sub>f</sub> = 5%. Consider a one-year European call with strike K = 180. The firm's own analyst, who follows the technology closely, puts the probability of the up state at 0.75, and the board wants that number used.</p><p>(a) Price the call by replication and again with the risk-neutral probability ρ. (3 points)</p><p>(b) Now do what the board asks. Compute the expected share price in one year and the expected return on the share, then the expected call payoff. Value the call by discounting that expected payoff first at r<sub>f</sub> and then at the share's expected return, and say why each answer is wrong. Finally find the discount rate that does give the right price, and confirm it with the relevering formula from chapter 4. (3 points)</p><p>(c) In four sentences, explain why the analyst's 0.75 never appears in the option's price, and say where the market's beliefs have gone. (2 points)</p>",
      solution: "<p><b>(a) Two routes, one price.</b> Bound check: 120.00 &lt; 1.05 × 160.00 = 168.00 &lt; 240.00 ✓ Payoffs: C<sub>u</sub> = max(240.00 − 180, 0) = 60.00 and C<sub>d</sub> = max(120.00 − 180, 0) = 0.00.</p><p>Δ = (60.00 − 0.00)/(240.00 − 120.00) = 60.00/120.00 = <b>0.5000</b><br>B = (0.00 − 120.00 × 0.5000)/1.05 = −60.00/1.05 = <b>−57.1429</b><br>C = 160.00 × 0.5000 − 57.1429 = 80.0000 − 57.1429 = <b>22.8571</b></p><p>State check: up, 0.5000 × 240.00 − 60.00 = 120.00 − 60.00 = 60.00 ✓; down, 0.5000 × 120.00 − 60.00 = 60.00 − 60.00 = 0.00 ✓ The repayment is 60.00 in both states because B is a fixed loan, which is exactly why the portfolio can track the option.</p><p>ρ = [(1.05)(160.00) − 120.00]/(240.00 − 120.00) = (168.00 − 120.00)/120.00 = 48.00/120.00 = <b>0.4000</b>, so 1 − ρ = 0.6000.<br>Check: [0.4000 × 240.00 + 0.6000 × 120.00]/1.05 = (96.0000 + 72.0000)/1.05 = 168.0000/1.05 = 160.00 ✓<br>C<sub>0</sub> = [0.4000 × 60.00 + 0.6000 × 0.00]/1.05 = 24.0000/1.05 = <b>22.8571</b> ✓</p><p>Note that ρ = 0.4000 sits well below the analyst's 0.7500. That gap is not a disagreement about the world. It is the risk adjustment: the call pays off in the good state, so the pricing weights shade that state down.</p><p><b>(b) What the real probability does and does not do.</b></p><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Computation</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Expected share price at date 1</td><td>0.7500 × 240.00 + 0.2500 × 120.00 = 180.0000 + 30.0000</td><td class=\"n\">210.0000</td></tr><tr><td>Expected return on the share, r<sub>S</sub></td><td>210.0000/160.00 − 1</td><td class=\"n\">0.3125 = 31.2500%</td></tr><tr><td>Expected call payoff</td><td>0.7500 × 60.00 + 0.2500 × 0.00</td><td class=\"n\">45.0000</td></tr><tr><td>Discounted at r<sub>f</sub></td><td>45.0000/1.05</td><td class=\"n\">42.8571 — wrong</td></tr><tr><td>Discounted at the share's 31.2500%</td><td>45.0000/1.3125</td><td class=\"n\">34.2857 — also wrong</td></tr><tr><td>The call's own expected return</td><td>45.0000/22.857143 − 1</td><td class=\"n\">0.968750 = 96.8750%</td></tr><tr><td>Discounted at 96.8750%</td><td>45.0000/1.968750</td><td class=\"n\">22.8571 ✓</td></tr></table><p><b>Why 42.8571 is wrong.</b> It pairs a real probability with the risk-free rate, which discounts a risky payoff as if it were certain. It overstates the call by 42.8571 − 22.8571 = 20.0000, almost double. This is the single most punished error in this material: the only probability entitled to r<sub>f</sub> is ρ.</p><p><b>Why 34.2857 is wrong.</b> It uses the right kind of rate but the wrong one. The replicating portfolio is 80.0000 of shares financed by 57.1429 of borrowing against 22.8571 of your own money, so the call is a levered position in the share with D/E = 57.142857/22.857143 = 2.5000. A levered claim is riskier than the asset, so 31.2500% is far too low.</p><p><b>The rate that works, and the circularity.</b> Relever the share's expected return exactly as in chapter 4, with the option playing the part of equity:<br>r<sub>C</sub> = r<sub>f</sub> + (S<sub>0</sub>Δ/C)(r<sub>S</sub> − r<sub>f</sub>) = 0.0500 + (80.0000/22.857143)(0.3125 − 0.0500) = 0.0500 + 3.500000 × 0.262500 = 0.0500 + 0.918750 = <b>0.968750</b> ✓<br>The multiplier 3.500000 is 1 + D/E = 1 + 2.5000 ✓, and 45.0000/1.968750 = 22.8571 returns the price from (a) ✓ So discounting the true expected payoff does work, but only at 96.8750%, and you cannot know that rate until you already know the price you were trying to compute. That circularity is the practical reason the risk-neutral route wins.</p><p><b>(c) Four sentences.</b> The replicating portfolio matches the call in every state, not on average, so two investors who violently disagree about the analyst's 0.75 must still agree that the call is half a share minus a loan and therefore worth 22.8571. Because the match is state by state, beliefs and risk preferences cancel out of the comparison entirely, and a probability that cancels cannot enter the price. The market's beliefs have not disappeared: they are already inside S<sub>0</sub> = 160.00, together with the risk premium investors demand, and the calculation uses them through that observed price rather than through a number anyone has to guess. ρ = 0.4000 is therefore a pricing weight, the probability that would make the share earn exactly r<sub>f</sub>, and presenting it as a forecast of the up state is a claim about the world that the prices do not support.</p>",
      criteria: [
        "Prices the call twice and gets 22.8571 both times: Δ = 0.5000 with B = −57.1429, and ρ = 0.4000 with the payoff discounted at r_f.",
        "Computes ρ with S_0 grown at the risk-free rate first, 1.05 × 160 = 168, not 160. Using 160 gives (160 − 120)/120 = 0.3333 and a call of 19.0476, and that certainty-equivalent slip is the error the solution keys name explicitly.",
        "Treats the analyst's 0.75 as a distractor in (a) and never mixes it with r_f. Doing so gives 45.0000/1.05 = 42.8571, an overstatement of 20.0000, which is the most heavily punished error in this topic.",
        "In (b) gets E[S_1] = 210.0000, r_S = 31.2500% and E[C_1] = 45.0000, and explains WHY both 42.8571 and 34.2857 are wrong: the first discounts a risky payoff at the risk-free rate, the second discounts a levered claim at the underlying asset's rate.",
        "Finds r_C = 96.8750% and confirms it by relevering, 0.0500 + 3.500000 × 0.262500, with S_0Δ/C = 3.5 recognised as 1 + D/E. Names the circularity: the correct rate is only knowable once the price is known.",
        "The verbal answer keeps to four sentences and contains the three moving parts: exact state-by-state replication makes beliefs cancel, the market's beliefs and risk aversion already sit inside S_0 = 160, and ρ is a pricing weight rather than a forecast."
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "k22-3",
      open: true,
      topic: "Two-period tree",
      points: 10,
      body: "<p>Ålesund Marine ASA trades at S<sub>0</sub> = 80.00 (all figures in NOK). Over each of the next two years the share is multiplied by u = 1.25 or by d = 0.75, so the tree recombines. The risk-free rate is r<sub>f</sub> = 5% per year, flat. Consider a put on the share with strike K = 85 expiring at date 2.</p><p>(a) Build the share tree and compute the risk-neutral probability ρ. Verify that ρ prices the share correctly at the up node at date 1. (2 points)</p><p>(b) Value the put as a European option by backward induction. Confirm the answer with the direct formula that uses the three terminal payoffs and the weights ρ², 2ρ(1 − ρ) and (1 − ρ)². (3 points)</p><p>(c) Value the same put as an American option. Write both candidates at every intermediate node and say which one you take. State the value of the right to exercise early. (3 points)</p><p>(d) Give the hedge ratio for the American put today and after each date-1 move, and explain in 2–3 sentences what rebalancing means here and why Δ changes. (2 points)</p>",
      solution: "<p><b>(a) The tree and ρ.</b> S<sub>u</sub> = 80.00 × 1.25 = 100.00 and S<sub>d</sub> = 80.00 × 0.75 = 60.00; then S<sub>uu</sub> = 125.00, S<sub>ud</sub> = S<sub>du</sub> = 75.00 and S<sub>dd</sub> = 45.00. Up-then-down and down-then-up both land on 75.00, which is what recombination means and why the tree stays small.</p><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Date 0</th><th>Date 1</th><th>Date 2</th></tr><tr><td class=\"n\">S<sub>0</sub> = 80.00</td><td class=\"n\">S<sub>u</sub> = 100.00</td><td class=\"n\">S<sub>uu</sub> = 125.00</td></tr><tr><td class=\"n\"></td><td class=\"n\"></td><td class=\"n\">S<sub>ud</sub> = 75.00</td></tr><tr><td class=\"n\"></td><td class=\"n\">S<sub>d</sub> = 60.00</td><td class=\"n\">S<sub>dd</sub> = 45.00</td></tr></table><p>Bound check at the root: 60.00 &lt; 1.05 × 80.00 = 84.00 &lt; 100.00 ✓</p><p>ρ = [(1.05)(80.00) − 60.00]/(100.00 − 60.00) = (84.00 − 60.00)/40.00 = 24.00/40.00 = <b>0.6000</b>, so 1 − ρ = 0.4000. Because u, d and r<sub>f</sub> are the same at every node, ρ is the same at every node.</p><p>Verify at the up node, S = 100.00, whose successors are 125.00 and 75.00: [0.6000 × 125.00 + 0.4000 × 75.00]/1.05 = (75.0000 + 30.0000)/1.05 = 105.0000/1.05 = <b>100.00</b> ✓</p><p><b>(b) The European put.</b> Terminal payoffs, taken at date 2:<br>P<sub>uu</sub> = max(85 − 125.00, 0) = 0.00<br>P<sub>ud</sub> = max(85 − 75.00, 0) = 10.00<br>P<sub>dd</sub> = max(85 − 45.00, 0) = 40.00</p><p>Roll back to date 1, one period at a time:<br>P<sub>u</sub> = [0.6000 × 0.00 + 0.4000 × 10.00]/1.05 = 4.0000/1.05 = <b>3.8095</b><br>P<sub>d</sub> = [0.6000 × 10.00 + 0.4000 × 40.00]/1.05 = (6.0000 + 16.0000)/1.05 = 22.0000/1.05 = <b>20.9524</b></p><p>Roll back to today, carrying the unrounded node values 3.809524 and 20.952381:<br>P<sub>0</sub> = [0.6000 × 3.809524 + 0.4000 × 20.952381]/1.05 = (2.285714 + 8.380952)/1.05 = 10.666667/1.05 = <b>10.1587</b></p><p><b>Check, the direct three-term formula.</b> The risk-neutral weights on the terminal nodes are ρ² = 0.3600, 2ρ(1 − ρ) = 0.4800 and (1 − ρ)² = 0.1600, which sum to 1.0000 ✓ Then<br>P<sub>0</sub> = [0.3600 × 0.00 + 0.4800 × 10.00 + 0.1600 × 40.00]/(1.05)² = (0.0000 + 4.8000 + 6.4000)/1.1025 = 11.2000/1.1025 = <b>10.1587</b> ✓<br>The factor 2 on the middle term is there because two paths reach 75.00. Forgetting it gives 7.9819, and the giveaway is that the weights then sum to 0.7600 rather than 1.0000: check that sum every time.</p><p><b>Second check, put-call parity.</b> Value the European call with the same strike on the same tree: C<sub>uu</sub> = 40.00, C<sub>ud</sub> = C<sub>dd</sub> = 0.00, so C<sub>u</sub> = 0.6000 × 40.00/1.05 = 24.0000/1.05 = 22.8571, C<sub>d</sub> = 0.0000, and C<sub>0</sub> = 0.6000 × 22.857143/1.05 = 13.714286/1.05 = 13.0612. Parity over two periods: C<sub>0</sub> − P<sub>0</sub> = 13.0612 − 10.1587 = 2.9025, while S<sub>0</sub> − PV(K) = 80.0000 − 85/(1.05)² = 80.0000 − 77.0975 = 2.9025 ✓ This one is a genuine check rather than an identity, because the call and the put were rolled back from different payoff vectors and parity was never used to produce either.</p><p><b>(c) The American put.</b> At every intermediate node compare continuation with immediate exercise and carry the larger figure backwards.</p><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Node</th><th>Continuation value</th><th>Exercise now</th><th>American value</th></tr><tr><td>Up, S = 100.00</td><td class=\"n\">[0.6(0.00) + 0.4(10.00)]/1.05 = 3.8095</td><td class=\"n\">max(85 − 100.00, 0) = 0.0000</td><td class=\"n\">3.8095 — hold</td></tr><tr><td>Down, S = 60.00</td><td class=\"n\">[0.6(10.00) + 0.4(40.00)]/1.05 = 20.9524</td><td class=\"n\">85 − 60.00 = 25.0000</td><td class=\"n\"><b>25.0000 — exercise</b></td></tr><tr><td>Today, S = 80.00</td><td class=\"n\">[0.6(3.809524) + 0.4(25.0000)]/1.05 = 11.7007</td><td class=\"n\">85 − 80.00 = 5.0000</td><td class=\"n\"><b>11.7007 — hold</b></td></tr></table><p>The root line in full: 2.285714 + 10.000000 = 12.285714, divided by 1.05, gives <b>11.7007</b>. Note that the down node now feeds 25.0000 into the root rather than 20.9524, which is the only place the American and European calculations differ.</p><p>Value of the right to exercise early = 11.700680 − 10.158730 = <b>1.5420</b>.</p><p><b>The mechanism, and the error it guards against.</b> Exercising at the down node hands you 85 − 60.00 = 25.00 a year early. Holding on is worth only 20.9524, for two reasons that both point the same way: the risk-neutral tree puts weight 0.6000 on the move back up to 75.00, where the put collapses to 10.00, and whatever the put does pay arrives a year later and is discounted again. Getting the strike early is exactly what an American put is for, and it is why early exercise bites for puts and never for a call on a share that pays no dividend. The expensive mistake is to discount the terminal payoffs all the way to the root with the weights from (b) and hand that in as the American value: it gives 10.1587, and no check inside the calculation complains, because 10.1587 is a perfectly correct European price. Write one line at each node showing both candidates and which one you took.</p><p><b>(d) Hedging and rebalancing.</b> The hedge ratio uses the values at the two nodes the position can reach, so for the American put today it uses 3.809524 and 25.000000:</p><p>Δ<sub>0</sub> = (3.809524 − 25.000000)/(100.00 − 60.00) = −21.190476/40.00 = <b>−0.5298</b>, carried as −0.529762 below<br>Δ<sub>u</sub> = (0.00 − 10.00)/(125.00 − 75.00) = −10.00/50.00 = <b>−0.2000</b><br>Δ<sub>d</sub> = (10.00 − 40.00)/(75.00 − 45.00) = −30.00/30.00 = <b>−1.0000</b>, which is academic for the American put because the holder exercises at that node and the hedge is closed out</p><p>Replication check at the root: B<sub>0</sub> = (P<sub>d</sub> − S<sub>d</sub>Δ<sub>0</sub>)/1.05 = (25.0000 + 60.00 × 0.529762)/1.05 = (25.0000 + 31.785714)/1.05 = 56.785714/1.05 = 54.081633, and P<sub>0</sub> = 80.00 × (−0.529762) + 54.081633 = −42.380952 + 54.081633 = <b>11.7007</b> ✓ Same as the backward induction, as it must be. Δ is negative, so the put is replicated by shorting shares and lending, the mirror image of a call.</p><p><b>What rebalancing means.</b> Today the hedge is short 0.5298 shares against 54.0816 lent; after an up move to 100.00 the position must be cut to short 0.2000 shares, and after a down move it would go to short a full share if the option were still alive. Δ measures how much the option's value moves when the share moves, and that sensitivity is not constant: an up move leaves the put paying in only one of its two remaining states, so the hedge flattens to −0.2000, while a down move leaves it in the money in both, so it tracks the share one for one at −1.0000. A hedger who set up 0.5298 shares and then did nothing would be wrongly hedged in both directions, and the adjustments are self-financing only if they are made, which is what makes real delta hedging expensive. For comparison, the European put's root hedge is (3.809524 − 20.952381)/40.00 = −0.4286, flatter than the American one because the American put is worth more at the down node.</p>",
      criteria: [
        "Builds the recombining tree 125.00 / 75.00 / 45.00 and says that up-then-down and down-then-up meet at 75.00. ρ = (1.05 × 80 − 60)/40 = 0.6000, with S_0 grown at r_f first: using 80 instead of 84 gives 0.5000 and is the certainty-equivalent error the keys call out. Notes that ρ is the same at every node because u, d and r_f are.",
        "European put by backward induction: P_u = 3.8095, P_d = 20.9524, P_0 = 10.1587, with the unrounded node values carried into the last step. Confirms with the weights 0.3600, 0.4800 and 0.1600, which must sum to 1; dropping the factor 2 on the middle term gives 7.9819 and leaves the weights summing to 0.7600.",
        "American put: compares continuation with immediate exercise at BOTH date-1 nodes, holds at 3.8095 in the up state and exercises at 25.0000 in the down state, then rolls back to 11.7007 and holds at the root. Early-exercise value 1.5420.",
        "Names the error of rolling an American option back as if it were European: it returns the perfectly correct European price 10.1587, and no internal check will complain. One line per node showing both candidates.",
        "Hedge ratios −0.5298 today, −0.2000 after an up move and −1.0000 after a down move, all as ratios of DIFFERENCES, with the sign negative because a put is replicated by shorting shares and lending. Verifies the root by replication: −42.3810 + 54.0816 = 11.7007.",
        "Explains rebalancing in 2–3 sentences: Δ is the option's sensitivity to the share, it is not constant, and the hedge must be adjusted at each node. Bonus for the parity check C_0 − P_0 = 13.0612 − 10.1587 = 2.9025 equalling 80 − 77.0975, which is a real check and not an identity."
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "k22-4",
      open: true,
      topic: "Backing out ρ",
      points: 8,
      body: "<p>Storfjord Kjemi AS is privately held, so there is no reliable quote on the share itself, but an over-the-counter market in one-year European options on it is active. Everyone agrees on the tree: in one year the share will be worth either S<sub>u</sub> = 140.00 or S<sub>d</sub> = 84.00 (all figures in NOK), and nothing else can happen. The one-year risk-free rate is r<sub>f</sub> = 5%. A one-year European call with strike K = 112 trades at 10.00.</p><p>(a) Back the risk-neutral probability out of the traded call, then use it to find the share price implied by the option market. Verify that your ρ reproduces both prices. (3 points)</p><p>(b) A client asks you to quote a one-year European put on the same share with strike K = 120. Price it, and confirm your price by replication. (3 points)</p><p>(c) A second one-year European call on the same share, strike K = 98, is quoted elsewhere at 14.50. Is that consistent with the first quote? If not, construct the arbitrage and state the profit. (2 points)</p>",
      solution: "<p><b>(a) Running the machinery in reverse.</b> On a two-state tree every asset must be priced by the same weights, so any observed price yields ρ: ρ = [X<sub>0</sub>(1 + r<sub>f</sub>) − X<sub>d</sub>]/(X<sub>u</sub> − X<sub>d</sub>). Put X = the traded call. Its payoffs are C<sub>u</sub> = max(140.00 − 112, 0) = 28.00 and C<sub>d</sub> = max(84.00 − 112, 0) = 0.00, so</p><p>ρ = [10.00 × 1.05 − 0.00]/(28.00 − 0.00) = 10.5000/28.00 = <b>0.3750</b>, and 1 − ρ = 0.6250.</p><p>Now price the share with the same weights, which is what the question is really asking for:<br>S<sub>0</sub> = [ρS<sub>u</sub> + (1 − ρ)S<sub>d</sub>]/(1 + r<sub>f</sub>) = [0.3750 × 140.00 + 0.6250 × 84.00]/1.05 = (52.5000 + 52.5000)/1.05 = 105.0000/1.05 = <b>100.00</b></p><p><b>Check 1, the forward direction.</b> Feed that share price back into the formula on the sheet: ρ = [(1.05)(100.00) − 84.00]/(140.00 − 84.00) = (105.00 − 84.00)/56.00 = 21.00/56.00 = 0.3750 ✓ The two directions are the same equation, so this locates arithmetic slips rather than proving the tree right.</p><p><b>Check 2, replication of the traded call.</b> Δ = 28.00/56.00 = 0.5000 and B = (0.00 − 84.00 × 0.5000)/1.05 = −42.00/1.05 = −40.0000, so C = 100.00 × 0.5000 − 40.0000 = 50.0000 − 40.0000 = 10.00 ✓ the observed quote.</p><p><b>Be honest about what those checks do.</b> Both of them are the same equation read in another direction, because the share price 100.00 was itself implied from ρ, and ρ from the quote of 10.00. They catch arithmetic slips, which is worth the two lines, but neither can tell you that the tree is right. The check that <i>can</i> fail is the one in (c), where a second observed price has to imply the same ρ and does not.</p><p><b>Bound check.</b> 84.00 &lt; 105.00 &lt; 140.00 ✓, equivalently 0 &lt; ρ &lt; 1 ✓ The two conditions are the same statement.</p><p><b>(b) The put, K = 120.</b> P<sub>u</sub> = max(120 − 140.00, 0) = 0.00 and P<sub>d</sub> = max(120 − 84.00, 0) = 36.00.</p><p>P<sub>0</sub> = [0.3750 × 0.00 + 0.6250 × 36.00]/1.05 = 22.5000/1.05 = <b>21.4286</b></p><p>Replication check, the independent route: Δ<sub>P</sub> = (0.00 − 36.00)/(140.00 − 84.00) = −36.00/56.00 = −0.642857, short shares as a put requires; B<sub>P</sub> = (P<sub>d</sub> − S<sub>d</sub>Δ<sub>P</sub>)/1.05 = (36.00 + 84.00 × 0.642857)/1.05 = (36.0000 + 54.0000)/1.05 = 90.0000/1.05 = 85.714286, positive, so you lend. P = 100.00 × (−0.642857) + 85.714286 = −64.285714 + 85.714286 = <b>21.4286</b> ✓</p><p>State by state: up, −0.642857 × 140.00 + 90.0000 = −90.0000 + 90.0000 = 0.00 ✓; down, −0.642857 × 84.00 + 90.0000 = −54.0000 + 90.0000 = 36.00 ✓</p><p><b>Bonus check, put-call parity.</b> A call struck at 120 is worth [0.3750 × 20.00]/1.05 = 7.5000/1.05 = 7.1429, and PV(K) = 120/1.05 = 114.2857, so P + S<sub>0</sub> − PV(K) = 21.4286 + 100.0000 − 114.2857 = 7.1429 ✓</p><p><b>What you have not said.</b> ρ = 0.3750 is a statement about prices, not about the world. Writing that there is a 37.5% chance the share reaches 140.00 is a different claim, and nothing in the data supports it: the observed prices contain the market's beliefs and its risk aversion fused together, and no amount of algebra separates them. For a claim that pays off in good states, ρ sits below the true probability.</p><p><b>(c) Two prices, one tree.</b> The second call pays C<sub>u</sub> = 140.00 − 98 = 42.00 and C<sub>d</sub> = 0.00. Price it with the ρ you already have:</p><p>Fair value = [0.3750 × 42.00]/1.05 = 15.7500/1.05 = <b>15.00</b>, against a quote of 14.50. It is <b>too cheap by 0.50</b>.</p><p>Equivalently, back ρ out of the second quote: ρ' = [14.50 × 1.05]/42.00 = 15.2250/42.00 = 0.3625 ≠ 0.3750. Two assets on the same two states imply different risk-neutral weights, so one of them is mispriced and there is an arbitrage. That is itself a legitimate answer to &laquo;comment on these prices&raquo;.</p><p><b>The trade.</b> You do not even need the share. The K = 98 call pays 42.00 or 0.00 and the K = 112 call pays 28.00 or 0.00, so 42.00/28.00 = 1.5000 of the K = 112 calls replicate the K = 98 call exactly, in both states:</p><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Leg</th><th>Action today</th><th>Cash today</th><th>Up, 140.00</th><th>Down, 84.00</th></tr><tr><td>Cheap call</td><td>Buy one K = 98 call at 14.50</td><td class=\"n\">−14.50</td><td class=\"n\">+42.00</td><td class=\"n\">0.00</td></tr><tr><td>Dear calls</td><td>Write 1.5 K = 112 calls at 10.00</td><td class=\"n\">+15.00</td><td class=\"n\">−42.00</td><td class=\"n\">0.00</td></tr><tr><td><b>Net</b></td><td></td><td class=\"n\"><b>+0.50</b></td><td class=\"n\"><b>0.00</b></td><td class=\"n\"><b>0.00</b></td></tr></table><p><b>Check:</b> the profit 0.50 equals the mispricing 15.00 − 14.50 = 0.50 ✓ Direction test: the K = 98 call was too cheap, so you must be buying it. The same trade written with the share instead gives the same number: Δ = 42.00/56.00 = 0.7500 and B = −84.00 × 0.7500/1.05 = −60.0000, so the replicating cost is 75.0000 − 60.0000 = 15.00, and buying the call at 14.50 while shorting that portfolio again nets 0.50 ✓</p>",
      criteria: [
        "Inverts the pricing equation rather than guessing: ρ = 10.00 × 1.05/28 = 0.3750 out of the traded call, with the observed price grown at r_f in the numerator. Using 10.00 instead of 10.50 gives 0.3571 and is the certainty-equivalent error the keys call out.",
        "Uses ρ to imply the share price, S_0 = (0.3750 × 140 + 0.6250 × 84)/1.05 = 100.00, and checks it by feeding 100.00 back through the formula-sheet version of ρ and by replicating the traded call as 50.00 − 40.00 = 10.00.",
        "Prices the K = 120 put at 21.4286 and confirms it independently by replication: Δ_P = −0.642857, short shares, and B_P = +85.714286, lending. A positive Δ for a put is a sign error and the classic way to lose these points.",
        "Never converts ρ into a claim about the world: saying there is a 37.5% chance of the up state is a statement about probability, not about prices, and the data do not support it. No real probability is invented or used anywhere, and none is needed.",
        "Spots the inconsistency in (c) by either route: the fair value 15.00 against a quote of 14.50, or a second implied ρ of 0.3625 against 0.3750. Says that two different implied weights on the same tree mean an arbitrage.",
        "Builds the arbitrage from the two calls, 1.5 of the K = 112 calls replicating the K = 98 call, buys the cheap one, shows zero payoff in both states, and checks that the profit 0.50 equals the mispricing. No calculation, no points."
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "k22-5",
      open: true,
      topic: "Comparative statics",
      points: 6,
      body: "<p>Tveita Teknologi ASA trades at S<sub>0</sub> = 300.00 (all figures in NOK). In one year the share will be worth either S<sub>u</sub> = 384.00 or S<sub>d</sub> = 234.00. Consider one-year European options on the share with strike K = 330.</p><p>(a) Compute ρ at r<sub>f</sub> = 4% and again at r<sub>f</sub> = 8%. Show that ρ is linear in r<sub>f</sub> with slope S<sub>0</sub>/(S<sub>u</sub> − S<sub>d</sub>), and use that to state the range of r<sub>f</sub> for which this tree admits no arbitrage. (2 points)</p><p>(b) Value the call and the put at both rates. Explain through the replicating portfolio why one rises and the other falls, and run put-call parity at both rates. (2 points)</p><p>(c) A colleague reads ρ = 0.5200 as &laquo;the market puts a 52% chance on the up state&raquo; and concludes that &laquo;if rates rise to 8% the market has turned more optimistic about the share&raquo;. In 4–6 sentences, say what is wrong with each half of that. (2 points)</p>",
      solution: "<p><b>(a) ρ as a function of the rate.</b> S<sub>u</sub> − S<sub>d</sub> = 384.00 − 234.00 = 150.00 throughout.</p><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>r<sub>f</sub></th><th>(1 + r<sub>f</sub>)S<sub>0</sub></th><th>Numerator</th><th>ρ</th><th>1 − ρ</th></tr><tr><td class=\"n\">4%</td><td class=\"n\">312.00</td><td class=\"n\">312.00 − 234.00 = 78.00</td><td class=\"n\"><b>0.5200</b></td><td class=\"n\">0.4800</td></tr><tr><td class=\"n\">8%</td><td class=\"n\">324.00</td><td class=\"n\">324.00 − 234.00 = 90.00</td><td class=\"n\"><b>0.6000</b></td><td class=\"n\">0.4000</td></tr></table><p>Check both: [0.5200 × 384.00 + 0.4800 × 234.00]/1.04 = (199.6800 + 112.3200)/1.04 = 312.0000/1.04 = 300.00 ✓ and [0.6000 × 384.00 + 0.4000 × 234.00]/1.08 = (230.4000 + 93.6000)/1.08 = 324.0000/1.08 = 300.00 ✓</p><p>Write ρ out as a function of r<sub>f</sub>: ρ = [S<sub>0</sub> − S<sub>d</sub> + r<sub>f</sub>S<sub>0</sub>]/(S<sub>u</sub> − S<sub>d</sub>) = (66.00 + 300.00r<sub>f</sub>)/150.00 = 0.4400 + 2.0000 r<sub>f</sub>. It is a straight line with intercept 0.4400, the value at r<sub>f</sub> = 0, and slope</p><p>dρ/dr<sub>f</sub> = S<sub>0</sub>/(S<sub>u</sub> − S<sub>d</sub>) = 300.00/150.00 = <b>2.0000</b></p><p>so each percentage point on the rate adds 0.0200 to ρ. Check against the table: 0.6000 − 0.5200 = 0.0800 = 2.0000 × 0.0400 ✓</p><p><b>The no-arbitrage range.</b> The condition S<sub>d</sub> &lt; (1 + r<sub>f</sub>)S<sub>0</sub> &lt; S<sub>u</sub> is exactly the condition 0 &lt; ρ &lt; 1, and it pins the rate: 234.00 &lt; 300.00(1 + r<sub>f</sub>) &lt; 384.00, that is <b>−22% &lt; r<sub>f</sub> &lt; 28%</b>. At the lower end ρ = 0 and the share can never lose to the bond; at the upper end ρ = 1 and it can never beat it. Outside the range the model does not return a bad price, it returns no price at all, because the tree is an arbitrage before any option is written on it.</p><p><b>(b) The two options at both rates.</b> Payoffs: C<sub>u</sub> = max(384.00 − 330, 0) = 54.00, C<sub>d</sub> = 0.00; P<sub>u</sub> = 0.00, P<sub>d</sub> = max(330 − 234.00, 0) = 96.00.</p><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>r<sub>f</sub></th><th>Call, [ρ × 54.00]/(1 + r<sub>f</sub>)</th><th>Put, [(1 − ρ) × 96.00]/(1 + r<sub>f</sub>)</th></tr><tr><td class=\"n\">4%</td><td class=\"n\">28.0800/1.04 = <b>27.0000</b></td><td class=\"n\">46.0800/1.04 = <b>44.3077</b></td></tr><tr><td class=\"n\">8%</td><td class=\"n\">32.4000/1.08 = <b>30.0000</b></td><td class=\"n\">38.4000/1.08 = <b>35.5556</b></td></tr></table><p><b>Why, through the replicating portfolio.</b> The hedge ratios do not depend on r<sub>f</sub> at all, because they are built from date-1 numbers only: Δ<sub>C</sub> = 54.00/150.00 = 0.3600 and Δ<sub>P</sub> = −96.00/150.00 = −0.6400 at both rates. What moves is the financing leg.</p><p>Call: S<sub>0</sub>Δ<sub>C</sub> = 108.0000 in both cases, and B = −234.00 × 0.3600/(1 + r<sub>f</sub>) = −84.2400/(1 + r<sub>f</sub>), which is −81.0000 at 4% and −78.0000 at 8%. The call is a levered long position in the share, and a higher rate means you need to borrow less today to repay the same 84.2400 next year, so the package costs more: 108.0000 − 81.0000 = 27.0000 and 108.0000 − 78.0000 = 30.0000 ✓</p><p>Put: S<sub>0</sub>Δ<sub>P</sub> = −192.0000 in both cases, and B = (96.00 + 149.7600)/(1 + r<sub>f</sub>) = 245.7600/(1 + r<sub>f</sub>), which is 236.3077 at 4% and 227.5556 at 8%. The put is a short share position financed by lending, and a higher rate means you need to lend less today to have 245.7600 next year, so the package costs less: −192.0000 + 236.3077 = 44.3077 and −192.0000 + 227.5556 = 35.5556 ✓</p><p>Said in ρ language, a higher rate does two things: it shifts weight onto the up state, 0.5200 to 0.6000, and it discounts harder. For the put, whose payoff sits in the down state, the two effects push the same way and the price must fall. For the call the two effects pull against each other, the weight helping and the discounting hurting, so the direction is not obvious from the formula alone, and that is precisely why the replicating-portfolio argument above is the one to write down: Δ is fixed, only the borrowing is repriced, and the call must rise.</p><p><b>Parity, at both rates.</b> 4%: C − P = 27.0000 − 44.3077 = −17.3077, and S<sub>0</sub> − PV(K) = 300.0000 − 330/1.04 = 300.0000 − 317.3077 = −17.3077 ✓ 8%: C − P = 30.0000 − 35.5556 = −5.5556, and 300.0000 − 330/1.08 = 300.0000 − 305.5556 = −5.5556 ✓ Parity is a genuine check here: the call and the put came from different payoff vectors and different hedge ratios, and it would catch a sign error in Δ<sub>P</sub> or a strike discounted at the wrong rate.</p><p><b>(c) What is wrong with both halves.</b> First half: ρ is not anybody's probability. It is the weight that makes this share earn exactly r<sub>f</sub> in expectation, which is a statement about the price 300.00, not about the world, and the true probability cannot be recovered from the tree at all, because S<sub>0</sub> fuses the market's beliefs with its risk aversion and no algebra separates them. For a share, whose payoff is high in good states, ρ sits below the true probability, so 0.5200 is if anything a lower bound on what the market believes. Second half is worse, because nothing in the comparative static involves an opinion: S<sub>0</sub>, S<sub>u</sub> and S<sub>d</sub> were held fixed and only the financing rate changed, so by construction nobody's view of the share moved. ρ had to rise to 0.6000 for the arithmetic to keep working, since the share must still be worth 300.00 today when its risk-neutral expectation is discounted at the higher 8%. The sentence to write instead is that a higher r<sub>f</sub> raises ρ mechanically, at the rate S<sub>0</sub>/(S<sub>u</sub> − S<sub>d</sub>) = 2.0000 per unit of r<sub>f</sub>, and that this is a repricing of the financing leg, not a change of sentiment.</p>",
      criteria: [
        "ρ = 0.5200 at 4% and 0.6000 at 8%, both with S_0 grown at the rate first: 312 and 324, never 300. Using 300 gives 0.4400 at every rate, which is the value at a zero rate and the certainty-equivalent error the keys call out. Checks that each ρ reprices the share at 300.00.",
        "Derives ρ = 0.4400 + 2.0000 r_f, identifies the slope as S_0/(S_u − S_d) = 2.0000, and confirms it against the table: 0.6000 − 0.5200 = 2.0000 × 0.0400.",
        "States the range from the no-arbitrage bound, minus 22% up to 28%, and says that this is the same condition as ρ lying strictly between 0 and 1: outside it the tree is an arbitrage before any option exists.",
        "Call 27.0000 rising to 30.0000, put 44.3077 falling to 35.5556, with the numerators shown.",
        "Explains the direction through the replicating portfolio rather than by assertion: Δ_C = 0.3600 and Δ_P = −0.6400 are unchanged by the rate, and only the present value of the financing leg moves, the borrowing for the call and the lending for the put. Equivalent explanation through the two ρ channels is accepted.",
        "Runs put-call parity at both rates and gets −17.3077 and −5.5556 on both sides. In (c), keeps to 4–6 sentences and makes both points: ρ is a pricing weight and not a forecast, and a rate change is a repricing of financing, not a change in sentiment."
      ]
    }
  ]
};

/* Chapter 25 — Real options */
window.EDU_DATA.chapterTasks[25] = {
  minutes: 72,
  tasks: [
    {
      id: "k25-1",
      open: true,
      topic: "Option to wait",
      points: 7,
      body: "<p>Fjordkraft Vind AS can build a wind farm today for an investment of 800 (all figures in NOK million), payable at once. Once built, the farm produces a net cash flow every year forever, starting one year after it is built. The level of that cash flow depends on a tariff ruling that will be announced in exactly one year: with probability 0.60 the tariff is generous and the farm pays 120 a year, with probability 0.40 it is tight and the farm pays 50 a year. The regime, once announced, is permanent. The cost of capital is r = 8%.</p><p>Instead of building today, the firm may wait one year, observe the ruling, and then decide. If it builds at date 1 the investment cost is still 800 and the cash flows start one year after that. No competitor can take the site in the meantime.</p><p>(a) What is the NPV of building today, and what does the plain NPV rule say? (2 points)</p><p>(b) Draw the decision tree and compute the value today of the strategy \"wait one year, then decide\". Should the firm build now or wait? (3 points)</p><p>(c) What is the value of the option to wait? Decompose it into the two effects that waiting has, and explain in 2–3 sentences why a positive NPV today does not settle the question. (2 points)</p>",
      solution: "<p><b>(a) Build today.</b> Building now commits the firm to both regimes, so the relevant cash flow is the expected one: E[CF] = 0.60 × 120 + 0.40 × 50 = 72 + 20 = 92 a year, a perpetuity starting at date 1.</p><p>NPV<sub>now</sub> = 92/0.08 − 800 = 1150 − 800 = <b>+350</b>. The plain NPV rule says build today.</p><p><b>(b) The tree, and the value of waiting.</b> In words: a decision node (square) at date 0 with two branches, build now or wait. On the build-now branch a chance node (circle) follows at date 1, generous with 0.60 or tight with 0.40, but the 800 is already spent, so no decision remains and the branch is worth the average of the two outcomes. On the wait branch the chance node comes first, and a second decision node follows in each state. That second square, placed after the circle, is where the option lives.</p><p>State-by-state NPVs, measured at the date the plant is built. They have the same size at date 1 as at date 0, because cost and cash flows shift together:</p><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>State</th><th>p<sub>i</sub></th><th>Cash flow</th><th>NPV<sub>i</sub> = CF/0.08 − 800</th><th>Decision at date 1</th></tr><tr><td>Generous</td><td class=\"n\">0.60</td><td class=\"n\">120</td><td class=\"n\">1500 − 800 = +700</td><td>build</td></tr><tr><td>Tight</td><td class=\"n\">0.40</td><td class=\"n\">50</td><td class=\"n\">625 − 800 = −175</td><td>do not build, take 0</td></tr></table><p>Consistency check before building anything on top of these: investing today accepts both branches, 0.60 × 700 + 0.40 × (−175) = 420 − 70 = 350 = NPV<sub>now</sub>. ✓</p><p>Value of waiting = Σp<sub>i</sub> × max(0, NPV<sub>i</sub>)/(1 + r) = [0.60 × 700 + 0.40 × 0]/1.08 = 420/1.08 = <b>388.8889</b></p><p>388.8889 &gt; 350, so <b>wait</b>. The max is taken inside the tree, at each date-1 decision node, and only then is the expectation discounted, once, by 1.08.</p><p><b>(c) The option to wait.</b> Value of the option = value of waiting − max(0, NPV<sub>now</sub>) = 388.8889 − 350 = <b>38.8889</b>. This is the option to wait, an American call on the project struck at the investment cost of 800, and exercising it today by building throws away 38.8889 of value.</p><p>Decomposition into the two forces:</p><p>Avoiding the tight branch: 0.40 × 175 = +70.0000 (that branch's −175 was already a date-0 figure on the build-now branch).<br>Delaying the generous branch by a year: −(0.60 × 700 − 0.60 × 700/1.08) = −(420.0000 − 388.8889) = −31.1111.<br>Net: 70.0000 − 31.1111 = 38.8889 ✓, the same number by an independent route.</p><p>Why a positive NPV does not settle it: the NPV rule compares building today with never building, but the real alternative is building next year with the ruling known, and that alternative is worth 388.8889, more than 350. The option has value because all three ingredients are present: uncertainty about the tariff, information that arrives in a year, and an irreversible outlay. Remove any one of them, say a competitor who takes the site if the firm hesitates, and the right to wait is worth nothing and the firm should build today. Sanity check: the option's value is never negative; had waiting come out below 350, the firm would simply build today and the right to delay would be worth zero, not a negative number.</p>",
      criteria: [
        "States the method before the number: building today commits the firm to the expected cash flow, 92/0.08 − 800 = +350, and says what the plain NPV rule would do.",
        "Values each state at the date the plant is built, +700 and −175, and shows the check that they average back to 350. The NPV_i figures are discounted exactly once more, by 1.08, to reach today, never twice.",
        "Takes the max inside the tree: the tight state contributes 0, not −175. Value of waiting = 420/1.08 = 388.8889, and the decision WAIT is written in words.",
        "Option to wait = 388.8889 − max(0, 350) = 38.8889, reported as the difference between two strategies that were both computed.",
        "Decomposes it, +70 from avoiding the tight branch and −31.1111 from delaying the generous one, names the mechanism (the option to wait, a call on the project struck at 800) and the three ingredients: uncertainty, information arriving, irreversibility.",
        "Draws the tree with squares for decisions and circles for chance, and puts the second decision node after the chance node on the wait branch. No calculation, no points."
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "k25-2",
      open: true,
      topic: "Value of information",
      points: 9,
      body: "<p>Brimstad Foods is considering a product launch. The launch costs 400 today (all figures in NOK million), and the present value of the cash flows it generates depends on a demand state that the firm cannot currently observe:</p><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Demand</th><th>Probability</th><th>PV of cash flows</th></tr><tr><td>Strong</td><td class=\"n\">0.25</td><td class=\"n\">700</td></tr><tr><td>Base</td><td class=\"n\">0.45</td><td class=\"n\">430</td></tr><tr><td>Weak</td><td class=\"n\">0.30</td><td class=\"n\">250</td></tr></table><p>The cost of capital is r = 9%. A market research firm offers a study that would reveal the demand state with certainty.</p><p>(a) What is the expected NPV of launching without the study, and what does the firm do without it? (2 points)</p><p>(b) The study is delivered immediately. What is the most Brimstad should pay for it? Verify the answer by an independent route. (3 points)</p><p>(c) Suppose instead that the study takes one year, and that the launch and all its cash flows shift with it. What is the study worth now, and why has the value changed? (2 points)</p><p>(d) Return to the immediate study, but suppose the launch cost is 470 rather than 400. What is perfect information worth now? Explain in 2–3 sentences why it is worth more although the project is worse. (2 points)</p>",
      solution: "<p><b>(a) The uninformed decision.</b> NPV<sub>i</sub> = PV<sub>i</sub> − 400 state by state: strong +300, base +30, weak −150.</p><p>E[NPV] = 0.25 × 300 + 0.45 × 30 + 0.30 × (−150) = 75.0000 + 13.5000 − 45.0000 = <b>+43.5000</b>. Positive, so without the study the firm launches, taking all three states, and the launch is worth max(0, E[NPV]) = 43.5000.</p><p><b>(b) The value of perfect information.</b> With the study the firm decides state by state and declines the weak state:</p><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>State</th><th>p<sub>i</sub></th><th>NPV<sub>i</sub></th><th>Informed decision</th><th>p<sub>i</sub> × max(0, NPV<sub>i</sub>)</th></tr><tr><td>Strong</td><td class=\"n\">0.25</td><td class=\"n\">+300</td><td>launch</td><td class=\"n\">75.0000</td></tr><tr><td>Base</td><td class=\"n\">0.45</td><td class=\"n\">+30</td><td>launch</td><td class=\"n\">13.5000</td></tr><tr><td>Weak</td><td class=\"n\">0.30</td><td class=\"n\">−150</td><td>do not launch</td><td class=\"n\">0.0000</td></tr></table><p>E[max(0, NPV<sub>i</sub>)] = 75.0000 + 13.5000 + 0.0000 = <b>88.5000</b></p><p>Value of perfect information = E[max(0, NPV<sub>i</sub>)] − max(0, E[NPV]) = 88.5000 − 43.5000 = <b>45.0000</b>. That is the most Brimstad should pay. At any price above 45 it refuses, and since imperfect information is worth less than perfect information, 45 caps the value of any study whatever its seller promises.</p><p>Independent check: the information changes exactly one decision, in the weak state, where it stops a loss of 150. Its value must therefore be p<sub>weak</sub> × 150 = 0.30 × 150 = 45.0000 ✓. This check catches the commonest error, dropping the max(0, ·) so that the weak state contributes −45 to both terms and the value of information collapses to zero.</p><p><b>(c) The study takes a year.</b> Everything the study can teach arrives a year late, so the informed strategy must be discounted: 88.5000/1.09 = <b>81.1927</b>. The alternative is unchanged, launch today for 43.5000, because a firm that waits for the study gives that up.</p><p>Value of the delayed study = 81.1927 − 43.5000 = <b>37.6927</b></p><p>The fall is 45.0000 − 37.6927 = 7.3073, which is 88.5000 × (1 − 1/1.09) = 88.5000 × 0.0825688 = 7.3073 ✓, exactly one year's discounting on the informed strategy. The delayed study is the option to wait in another guise: the whole informed strategy, the 88.5 of strong-state and base-state NPV, is pushed back a year while the alternative of launching today is not, and that year is what the delay costs. A study priced at 40 is worth buying only if it can be delivered at once.</p><p><b>(d) Launch cost 470.</b> NPV<sub>i</sub> = PV<sub>i</sub> − 470: strong +230, base −40, weak −220.</p><p>E[NPV] = 0.25 × 230 + 0.45 × (−40) + 0.30 × (−220) = 57.5000 − 18.0000 − 66.0000 = <b>−26.5000</b>. Negative, so the uninformed firm does not launch, and the uninformed value is max(0, −26.5000) = 0, not −26.5.</p><p>E[max(0, NPV<sub>i</sub>)] = 0.25 × 230 + 0.45 × 0 + 0.30 × 0 = <b>57.5000</b></p><p>Value of perfect information = 57.5000 − 0 = <b>57.5000</b>, up from 45.0000.</p><p>Check: the information now changes the decision in the strong state only, from reject to launch, and it is worth p<sub>strong</sub> × 230 = 0.25 × 230 = 57.5000 ✓.</p><p>Why more: information has value only where it changes a decision. At a cost of 400 the uninformed firm launches, so the study's only job is to stop the weak-state loss, worth 0.30 × 150 = 45. At 470 the uninformed firm rejects, so the study's job is to rescue the strong state, worth 0.25 × 230 = 57.5. The project got worse, but the uninformed decision moved further from what an informed firm would do, and that gap is what the study is worth. When the uninformed decision is to reject, the study is worth the entire informed value, which is the form H2025 Exercise 5(b) took.</p>",
      criteria: [
        "Computes NPV_i = PV_i − 400 per state (+300, +30, −150), then E[NPV] = 43.5 and states the uninformed decision: launch.",
        "Writes the max inside the expectation: E[max(0, NPV_i)] = 75 + 13.5 + 0 = 88.5. The weak state contributes 0, not −45.",
        "Value of perfect information = 88.5 − 43.5 = 45, stated as a maximum willingness to pay, and checked by the independent route 0.30 × 150 = 45: information is worth exactly the loss it avoids in the one state where it changes the decision.",
        "Delayed study: discounts the informed strategy once, 88.5/1.09 = 81.1927, keeps the alternative at 43.5, and gets 37.6927. Explains the fall of 7.3073 as one year's discounting on the informed strategy, not on the difference.",
        "With a cost of 470 recognises that the uninformed value is max(0, −26.5) = 0, so the study is worth the full informed value 57.5, and explains that information is valuable in proportion to how wrong the uninformed decision is, not to how good the project is.",
        "Sanity checks stated: a study can never have negative value, and the perfect-information figure caps what any imperfect study can be worth."
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "k25-3",
      open: true,
      topic: "Exit option",
      points: 10,
      body: "<p>Havgap Shipping can buy a vessel today for 900 (all figures in NOK million). From date 1 the vessel earns a net cash flow every year forever: 130 a year if the charter market turns out strong, probability 0.50, or 30 a year if it turns out weak, probability 0.50. The state is revealed by the first year's cash flow and is then permanent. The cost of capital is r = 9%.</p><p>A buyer has committed to purchase the vessel for a fixed 450 at any date from date 3 onwards. The sale takes place immediately after that date's cash flow, and the vessel's cash flows then stop. Before date 3 a charter contract binds the vessel and no sale is possible.</p><p>(a) What is the NPV of buying the vessel if Havgap is committed to operating it forever? Would it buy? (2 points)</p><p>(b) Now take the sale into account. In which state is the vessel sold, and when? Compute the NPV of buying the vessel and say whether Havgap should buy. (4 points)</p><p>(c) What is the exit option worth? Verify by an independent route and name the kind of option it is. (2 points)</p><p>(d) The buyer offers to waive the three-year lock-in, so that the vessel could be sold for 450 at date 1, right after the first cash flow. What is the waiver worth to Havgap? (2 points)</p>",
      solution: "<p><b>(a) Committed to operating.</b> With no way out the firm holds the expected perpetuity: E[CF] = 0.50 × 130 + 0.50 × 30 = 65 + 15 = 80 a year.</p><p>PV = 80/0.09 = 888.8889, so NPV<sub>committed</sub> = 888.8889 − 900 = <b>−11.1111</b>. Negative: a committed buyer does not buy.</p><p>State by state, which everything below is built on: NPV<sub>strong</sub> = 130/0.09 − 900 = 1444.4444 − 900 = +544.4444 and NPV<sub>weak</sub> = 30/0.09 − 900 = 333.3333 − 900 = −566.6667. Check: 0.50 × 544.4444 + 0.50 × (−566.6667) = 272.2222 − 283.3333 = −11.1111 ✓.</p><p><b>(b) With the exit.</b> The decision is taken at date 3, when the state is known. At that date the vessel is worth either its continuation value, a perpetuity starting at date 4, or the sale price, whichever is larger:</p><p>Strong: continuing is worth 130/0.09 = 1444.4444 against 450, so keep operating. The strong branch is unchanged at +544.4444.<br>Weak: continuing is worth 30/0.09 = 333.3333 against 450, so sell at date 3.</p><p>The weak branch is no longer a perpetuity but a three-year annuity of 30 plus 450 received at date 3. The three-year annuity factor at 9% is A(3, 9%) = [1 − 1.09<sup>−3</sup>]/0.09 = 2.531295, and the three-year discount factor is 1.09<sup>−3</sup> = 0.772183.</p><p>PV<sub>weak</sub> = 30 × 2.531295 + 450 × 0.772183 = 75.9388 + 347.4826 = 423.4214<br>NPV<sub>weak</sub> = 423.4214 − 900 = −476.5786</p><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>State</th><th>p<sub>i</sub></th><th>Decision at date 3</th><th>NPV<sub>i</sub></th><th>p<sub>i</sub> × NPV<sub>i</sub></th></tr><tr><td>Strong</td><td class=\"n\">0.50</td><td>continue</td><td class=\"n\">+544.4444</td><td class=\"n\">+272.2222</td></tr><tr><td>Weak</td><td class=\"n\">0.50</td><td>sell for 450</td><td class=\"n\">−476.5786</td><td class=\"n\">−238.2893</td></tr></table><p>NPV with the exit = 272.2222 − 238.2893 = <b>+33.9329</b>. Positive, so <b>buy the vessel</b>. Note the timing: the 900 is paid in both states and the first three years of 30 are endured in the weak state, since the firm commits before it learns the state. Only the perpetuity beyond date 3 is truncated.</p><p><b>(c) The exit option.</b> Value = NPV with exit − NPV committed = 33.9329 − (−11.1111) = <b>45.0440</b>.</p><p>Independent check: the option changes one branch, at one date, by the difference between the sale price and what continuing was worth there: 0.50 × (450 − 333.3333) = 58.3333 at date 3, and 58.3333 × 0.772183 = 45.0440 today ✓.</p><p>This is an abandonment option, an exit option: a put on the vessel struck at the sale price of 450, exercisable from date 3. It raises the value of the project without changing a single operating cash flow, because it cuts off the left tail, and here it is what turns a project a committed buyer would refuse into one worth buying. Sanity check: the option's value is never negative, because in the strong state it is simply left unexercised.</p><p><b>(d) The waiver.</b> With the lock-in gone, the weak-state vessel is sold at date 1, right after the first cash flow of 30: PV<sub>weak</sub> = (30 + 450)/1.09 = 480/1.09 = 440.3670 and NPV<sub>weak</sub> = 440.3670 − 900 = −459.6330.</p><p>NPV = 0.50 × 544.4444 + 0.50 × (−459.6330) = 272.2222 − 229.8165 = 42.4057, so the waiver is worth 42.4057 − 33.9329 = <b>8.4728</b>.</p><p>Check from the weak branch alone. At date 1 the locked-in firm holds two more years of 30 and the 450 at date 3: 30/1.09 + 30/1.09<sup>2</sup> + 450/1.09<sup>2</sup> = 27.5229 + 25.2504 + 378.7560 = 431.5293. The waiver replaces that with 450 at once, a gain of 450 − 431.5293 = 18.4707 at date 1, which is 18.4707/1.09 = 16.9456 today, and it happens with probability 0.50: 0.50 × 16.9456 = 8.4728 ✓.</p><p>Ordering check on the weak branch, all measured at date 0: no exit 333.3333 &lt; exit at date 3 423.4214 &lt; exit at date 1 440.3670. A locked-in figure outside that range means the annuity or the discounting of the 450 is wrong.</p>",
      criteria: [
        "Committed case: values the expected perpetuity 80/0.09 = 888.8889, NPV = −11.1111, and says a committed buyer would not buy. Shows the state NPVs +544.4444 and −566.6667 averaging back to −11.1111.",
        "Compares continuation with the sale price at date 3 in each state: 1444.4444 > 450 so continue in the strong state, 333.3333 < 450 so sell in the weak state. The sale is exercised in one state only.",
        "Replaces the weak-state perpetuity with a truncated annuity plus the discounted sale price: 30 × 2.531295 + 450 × 0.772183 = 423.4214. The 900 is paid in both states and the three years of 30 are not skipped.",
        "NPV with exit = 0.50 × 544.4444 + 0.50 × (−476.5786) = +33.9329, and the decision BUY is stated in words.",
        "Option value 45.0440 as the difference between the two NPVs, verified as 0.50 × (450 − 333.3333) × 1.09^−3, and named: an abandonment option, a put on the vessel struck at 450, which adds value by cutting off the left tail.",
        "Waiver: weak branch (30 + 450)/1.09 = 440.3670, NPV 42.4057, waiver worth 8.4728, with the ordering check 333.3333 < 423.4214 < 440.3670."
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "k25-4",
      open: true,
      topic: "Indifference point",
      points: 6,
      body: "<p>Return to the wind farm of the first task, with the same data: investment 800 (NOK million), a perpetual cash flow of 120 a year with probability 0.60 or 50 a year with probability 0.40, starting one year after the plant is built, the tariff ruling announced in one year, r = 8%, and the option to wait one year at an unchanged investment cost. There you found NPV<sub>now</sub> = 350 and a value of waiting of 388.8889.</p><p>(a) At what investment cost I* is Fjordkraft indifferent between building today and waiting? State the interval in which your algebra is valid, verify the answer, and say which side of I* does what. (3 points)</p><p>(b) Return to I = 800. At what probability p* of the generous tariff is the firm indifferent between building today and waiting? Interpret the result in 2–3 sentences. (3 points)</p>",
      solution: "<p><b>(a) Solving for the investment cost.</b> Write both strategies as functions of I.</p><p>Build now: NPV<sub>now</sub>(I) = 92/0.08 − I = 1150 − I<br>Wait: [0.60 × max(0, 1500 − I) + 0.40 × max(0, 625 − I)]/1.08</p><p>Interval: for 625 &lt; I &lt; 1500 the generous state is worth building and the tight state is not, so the wait strategy is 0.60 × (1500 − I)/1.08. Below 625 both states would be built, above 1500 neither. The max operators make each strategy piecewise linear, so the algebra is valid inside one interval only.</p><p>Set the two equal:<br>1150 − I = 0.60 × (1500 − I)/1.08<br>1.08 × (1150 − I) = 0.60 × (1500 − I)<br>1242 − 1.08I = 900 − 0.60I<br>342 = 0.48I<br><b>I* = 712.5000</b></p><p>Verify: 712.5 lies in (625, 1500) ✓. Build now: 1150 − 712.5 = 437.5000. Wait: 0.60 × (1500 − 712.5)/1.08 = 0.60 × 787.5/1.08 = 472.5/1.08 = 437.5000 ✓, equal, as required.</p><p>Which side: below I* = 712.5 build today, above it wait. At the original 800 the firm waits, consistent with the first task ✓. The intuition is that a cheap plant has little to lose in the tight state (at I = 625 nothing at all), so the insurance that waiting provides is not worth a year of delay on the generous branch.</p><p><b>(b) Solving for the probability.</b> With I = 800 the state NPVs are +700 and −175 whatever p is, so the tight state is never built and the wait strategy has one live branch for every p. No interval on p needs checking for the max operators, but the alternative to waiting must be building, which requires NPV<sub>now</sub> ≥ 0 at the solution.</p><p>Build now: [p × 120 + (1 − p) × 50]/0.08 − 800 = (50 + 70p)/0.08 − 800 = 625 + 875p − 800 = 875p − 175<br>Wait: p × 700/1.08 = 648.1481p</p><p>Set equal:<br>875p − 175 = 648.1481p<br>226.8519p = 175<br><b>p* = 0.7714</b>, exactly 27/35.</p><p>Verify with the exact fraction: build now 875 × 27/35 − 175 = 675 − 175 = 500; wait 700 × 27/35 divided by 1.08 = 540/1.08 = 500 ✓. And NPV<sub>now</sub> = 500 &gt; 0 at p*, so the comparison is against building, not against doing nothing ✓ (had 875p − 175 been negative there, the right-hand side would have been 0 instead).</p><p>Independent route: the option to wait as a function of p is the avoided loss minus the delay cost, (1 − p) × 175 − p × 700 × (1 − 1/1.08) = 175 − 175p − 51.8519p = 175 − 226.8519p, which is zero at p = 175/226.8519 = 0.7714 ✓.</p><p>Interpretation: above p* = 0.7714 build today, below it wait, and at the given 0.60 the firm waits ✓. The option to wait is insurance against the tight state. As that state becomes unlikely the insurance is worth less, while the delay still costs a year of the generous cash flow, and at p* the two exactly cancel. In the limit p = 1 there is nothing to learn and waiting is pure loss, which is the general result that the option to wait is worthless when no state would be declined. Sanity check: p* must lie above 0.60, since at 0.60 waiting wins, and it does ✓.</p>",
      criteria: [
        "Writes both strategies as functions of the unknown before solving: 1150 − I against 0.60(1500 − I)/1.08, and states the interval 625 < I < 1500 in which that form of the wait strategy is valid.",
        "I* = 712.5, verified by computing both sides (437.5 each) and by checking that 712.5 lies inside the interval.",
        "Says which side does what: build below 712.5, wait above, consistent with waiting at I = 800.",
        "For the probability: build now = 875p − 175, wait = 648.1481p, p* = 0.7714, and checks that NPV_now is positive at p* so the alternative to waiting is building rather than doing nothing.",
        "Interprets: the option to wait is insurance against the tight state, worth less as that state becomes unlikely and worthless at p = 1. Reaching the same p* from the option value 175 − 226.8519p = 0 is the independent check.",
        "Shows the algebra line by line. An answer that states 712.5 or 0.7714 without the equation earns nothing."
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "k25-5",
      open: true,
      topic: "Risk-neutral valuation",
      points: 8,
      body: "<p>Vestfall Energi holds a one-year exclusive licence to build a small hydro plant. Building costs 95 (all figures in NOK million), payable when construction starts, either today or in one year. If the plant is built today, the present value of its future cash flows is 100. That value depends on next year's tariff ruling: if the plant is built in one year instead, the present value of its cash flows, measured at date 1, will be 130 under a favourable ruling and 80 under an unfavourable one. The risk-free rate is 4%. The firm's analyst estimates the true probability of a favourable ruling at 0.70, but nobody knows the appropriate risk-adjusted discount rate for the plant.</p><p>(a) What is the NPV of building today? (2 points)</p><p>(b) What is the risk-neutral probability of a favourable ruling? Verify it, and explain in 2–3 sentences why the analyst's 0.70 is not needed. (3 points)</p><p>(c) What is the licence worth today if the firm may wait, and what is the value of the option to wait? Verify by replication and state the decision: build now or wait. (3 points)</p>",
      solution: "<p><b>(a) Build today.</b> NPV<sub>now</sub> = 100 − 95 = <b>+5</b>. The plain NPV rule says build.</p><p><b>(b) The risk-neutral probability.</b> The underlying asset is the plant's cash-flow stream, worth S<sub>0</sub> = 100 today and S<sub>u</sub> = 130 or S<sub>d</sub> = 80 at date 1. The investment cost 95 is the strike, not part of the asset, so the tree is built on 100, 130 and 80, not on the NPVs. The data signal the method: a value with up and down moves and a risk-free rate means risk-neutral valuation, not real probabilities with a cost of capital.</p><p>ρ = [(1 + r<sub>f</sub>)S<sub>0</sub> − S<sub>d</sub>]/(S<sub>u</sub> − S<sub>d</sub>) = [1.04 × 100 − 80]/(130 − 80) = [104 − 80]/50 = 24/50 = <b>0.4800</b>, and 1 − ρ = 0.5200.</p><p>Check: under ρ the asset must earn the risk-free rate, [0.48 × 130 + 0.52 × 80]/1.04 = [62.4 + 41.6]/1.04 = 104/1.04 = 100 = S<sub>0</sub> ✓. Sanity: 0 &lt; ρ &lt; 1 ✓. The numerator grows S<sub>0</sub> at the risk-free rate first, 100 × 1.04 = 104, and only then subtracts S<sub>d</sub>. Using 100 instead of 104 gives ρ = 20/50 = 0.40, and that is the certainty-equivalent error the solution keys single out.</p><p>Why 0.70 is not needed: the licence's payoff can be replicated with a position in the plant's cash-flow stream and risk-free borrowing, so its price is fixed by no-arbitrage and cannot depend on anyone's forecast or risk aversion. ρ is not a forecast; it is the probability under which the underlying is priced correctly at r<sub>f</sub>. The true probability would only matter together with a risk-adjusted discount rate, which nobody knows. Combining 0.70 with r<sub>f</sub> would give 0.70 × 35/1.04 = 23.5577, which discounts a risky payoff at the risk-free rate and overstates the value.</p><p><b>(c) The licence as a call.</b> Waiting gives a call on the plant struck at 95, exercised only where it pays:</p><p>C<sub>u</sub> = max(130 − 95, 0) = 35<br>C<sub>d</sub> = max(80 − 95, 0) = 0, the firm simply does not build.</p><p>C<sub>0</sub> = [ρC<sub>u</sub> + (1 − ρ)C<sub>d</sub>]/(1 + r<sub>f</sub>) = [0.48 × 35 + 0.52 × 0]/1.04 = 16.8/1.04 = <b>16.1538</b></p><p>Replication check: Δ = (C<sub>u</sub> − C<sub>d</sub>)/(S<sub>u</sub> − S<sub>d</sub>) = 35/50 = 0.7000; B = (C<sub>d</sub> − S<sub>d</sub>Δ)/(1 + r<sub>f</sub>) = (0 − 80 × 0.70)/1.04 = −56/1.04 = −53.8462; C<sub>0</sub> = S<sub>0</sub>Δ + B = 70.0000 − 53.8462 = 16.1538 ✓. Two routes, one number.</p><p>Value of the option to wait = 16.1538 − max(0, NPV<sub>now</sub>) = 16.1538 − 5 = <b>11.1538</b>. Decision: <b>wait</b>. Held as an option the licence is worth 16.1538; exercised today it is worth 5, so building now destroys 11.1538.</p><p>The tree in words: a decision node at date 0, build (worth 5) or wait; on the wait branch a chance node at date 1, favourable (S = 130, build, payoff 35) or unfavourable (S = 80, do not build, payoff 0), weighted with ρ = 0.48 rather than 0.70 and discounted at 4%. This is the option to wait, and it is valuable here even though today's NPV is positive, because an unfavourable ruling would turn the plant into a loss of 15 that waiting avoids. Sanity check: the option's value is never negative; had C<sub>0</sub> come out below 5 the firm would build today and the right to delay would be worth zero.</p>",
      criteria: [
        "NPV of building today = 100 − 95 = +5, and says the plain rule would build.",
        "Builds the tree on the cash-flow stream (S_0 = 100, S_u = 130, S_d = 80) with 95 as the strike, not on the NPVs.",
        "ρ = (1.04 × 100 − 80)/(130 − 80) = 0.48, with S_0 grown at the risk-free rate before S_d is subtracted. Using 100 instead of 104 gives 0.40 and is the certainty-equivalent error the keys single out. Checks that the risk-neutral expectation discounted at 4% returns 100.",
        "Explains why 0.70 is not used: the payoff is replicable, so the price is set by no-arbitrage; the true probability would matter only with a risk-adjusted rate, which is unknown. Mixing 0.70 with r_f is the named error.",
        "C_u = 35, C_d = 0 (not −15), C_0 = 0.48 × 35/1.04 = 16.1538 discounted at r_f, verified by replication with Δ = 0.70 and B = −53.8462.",
        "Option to wait = 16.1538 − 5 = 11.1538, decision WAIT written in words, and the mechanism named: the licence is a call on the plant, and a positive NPV today does not mean build."
      ]
    }
  ]
};

/* Chapter 26 — Mergers and acquisitions */
window.EDU_DATA.chapterTasks[26] = {
  minutes: 72,
  tasks: [
    {
      id: "k26-1",
      open: true,
      topic: "Stock swap",
      points: 12,
      body: "<p>Vestkyst Shipping ASA (the acquirer) has 60 million shares trading at 90 NOK. It offers ER = 0.42 of its own shares for each share of Havbris Logistikk AS (the target), which has 25 million shares trading at 32 NOK. Management expects synergies with a present value of S = 300 million NOK. The prices are pre-announcement and the market had not anticipated the bid. All figures in millions of NOK unless stated.</p><p>(a) How many new shares are issued, what is the share price of the merged firm, and what fraction of the merged firm do Havbris's former shareholders own? (3 points)</p><p>(b) Compute the NPV of the deal for Vestkyst's shareholders and for Havbris's shareholders. Run the check that the two NPVs sum to the synergies, and confirm NPV<sub>A</sub> by an independent route. (4 points)</p><p>(c) What is the maximum exchange ratio Vestkyst can offer, and the minimum exchange ratio Havbris can accept? Show both. (3 points)</p><p>(d) What premium did Vestkyst offer, and what premium do Havbris's shareholders actually receive? Explain in one sentence why the two differ. (2 points)</p>",
      solution: "<p><b>Setup.</b> Stand-alone values: A = N<sub>A</sub> × P<sub>A</sub> = 60 × 90.00 = <b>5400.00</b> and T = N<sub>T</sub> × P<sub>T</sub> = 25 × 32.00 = <b>800.00</b>. The merged firm is worth A + T + S = 5400.00 + 800.00 + 300.00 = <b>6500.00</b>. Use the stand-alone values, never a post-announcement market capitalisation, which would already contain the synergy you are about to add.</p><p><b>(a) New shares, merged price, ownership.</b> x = ER × N<sub>T</sub> = 0.42 × 25 = <b>10.5000</b> million new shares, so the merged firm has N<sub>A</sub> + x = 60 + 10.5 = 70.5000 million shares. P<sub>new</sub> = (A + T + S)/(N<sub>A</sub> + x) = 6500.00/70.5000 = <b>92.1986 NOK</b>. The target's former holders own y = x/(N<sub>A</sub> + x) = 10.5/70.5 = <b>0.14893617</b>, that is 14.8936%.</p><p><b>(b) The two NPVs.</b><br>NPV<sub>A</sub> = (1 − y)(A + T + S) − A = 0.85106383 × 6500.00 − 5400.00 = 5531.9149 − 5400.00 = <b>+131.9149</b><br>NPV<sub>T</sub> = y(A + T + S) − T = 0.14893617 × 6500.00 − 800.00 = 968.0851 − 800.00 = <b>+168.0851</b></p><p><b>Check, the summation identity:</b> 131.9149 + 168.0851 = 300.0000 = S ✓ The deal divides the synergy and creates nothing in the division. This identity cannot fail if x and y are consistent (it is not one of the manual's five numbered checks, but the examiner runs it), so its job is to locate an arithmetic slip, not to prove the answer right.</p><p><b>Independent route to NPV<sub>A</sub>, the price-change identity:</b> NPV<sub>A</sub> = N<sub>A</sub>(P<sub>new</sub> − P<sub>A</sub>) = 60 × (92.1986 − 90.0000) = 60 × 2.1986 = 131.9149 ✓ This one can fail, because it never uses y: get y wrong and the two routes disagree. Split: Vestkyst keeps 131.9149/300 = 43.9716% of the synergy, Havbris takes 56.0284%. Both NPVs are positive, so both sets of shareholders should vote yes.</p><p><b>(c) The bargaining range.</b> The acquirer's NPV is zero at the formula-sheet bound:<br>ER<sub>max</sub> = [(T + S)/A] × (N<sub>A</sub>/N<sub>T</sub>) = (1100.00/5400.00) × (60/25) = 0.203704 × 2.4000 = <b>0.4889</b><br>Second form, the one to remember: ER<sub>max</sub> = (P<sub>T</sub> + S/N<sub>T</sub>)/P<sub>A</sub> = (32.00 + 300.00/25)/90.00 = 44.0000/90.00 = 0.4889 ✓ The most Vestkyst can pay per Havbris share is the target's own price plus the entire synergy per target share, converted into acquirer shares at the acquirer's price.<br>The target's NPV is zero when S moves from the numerator to the denominator:<br>ER<sub>min</sub> = [T/(A + S)] × (N<sub>A</sub>/N<sub>T</sub>) = (800.00/5700.00) × 2.4000 = 0.140351 × 2.4000 = <b>0.3368</b><br>The offered 0.42 sits inside [0.3368, 0.4889]. Check at ER<sub>max</sub>: x = 0.488889 × 25 = 12.2222, P<sub>new</sub> = 6500.00/72.2222 = 90.0000 = P<sub>A</sub>, so NPV<sub>A</sub> = 0 ✓ The acquirer breaks even exactly when its own share price is unmoved.</p><p><b>(d) Offered versus actual premium.</b><br>Offered premium = (ER × P<sub>A</sub>)/P<sub>T</sub> − 1 = (0.42 × 90.00)/32.00 − 1 = 37.8000/32.00 − 1 = 0.181250 = <b>18.1250%</b><br>Actual premium = (ER × P<sub>new</sub>)/P<sub>T</sub> − 1 = (0.42 × 92.1986)/32.00 − 1 = 38.7234/32.00 − 1 = 0.210106 = <b>21.0106%</b><br>Check: NPV<sub>T</sub> = T × actual premium = 800.00 × 0.21010638 = 168.0851 ✓, the same figure as in (b).</p><p><b>Why they differ.</b> Havbris's holders are paid in Vestkyst shares, a currency whose value the deal itself changes: the ratio between the two premiums is (1 + actual)/(1 + offered) = P<sub>new</sub>/P<sub>A</sub> = 92.1986/90.0000 = 1.0244, and by the price-change identity P<sub>new</sub> &gt; P<sub>A</sub> exactly when NPV<sub>A</sub> &gt; 0. Here the deal is positive-NPV for the acquirer, so the currency appreciates and the target receives more than it was offered; in an overpaid deal it would receive less. In a cash deal the two premiums coincide, because cash is cash.</p>",
      criteria: [
        "Uses the stand-alone values A = 5400 and T = 800 and adds the synergy once, so the merged firm is worth 6500. Substituting a post-announcement market value double counts S.",
        "Computes x = 10.5 million new shares and divides by the post-deal share count of 70.5 million, giving P_new = 92.1986 and y = 0.1489.",
        "Writes both NPV formulas out and gets NPV_A = +131.9149, NPV_T = +168.0851, then runs the check that they sum to S = 300 and labels it honestly: an identity that locates arithmetic slips. Bonus for the independent price-change route 60 × 2.1986, which can actually fail.",
        "ER_max = 0.4889 from the formula-sheet bound, ideally confirmed by the second form (P_T + S/N_T)/P_A, and ER_min = 0.3368 with S moved to the denominator. States that the offered 0.42 lies inside the range.",
        "Distinguishes the two premiums: offered 18.1250% valued at P_A, actual 21.0106% valued at P_new. The classic error is to compute the premium at the offered price only and report 18.125% as what the target receives.",
        "Names the mechanism: the target is paid in acquirer shares, a currency whose value the deal changes, so the actual premium exceeds the offered one exactly when NPV_A is positive. Every step shown: no calculation, no points."
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "k26-2",
      open: true,
      topic: "EPS accretion",
      points: 6,
      body: "<p>Skarv Elektro ASA earns 250 million a year on 50 million shares and trades at 100 NOK per share. Tind Kabel AS earns 60 million a year on 20 million shares and trades at 24 NOK. Skarv offers a 25% premium for Tind, paid entirely in Skarv shares. Assume there are no synergies at all: S = 0. Figures in millions of NOK.</p><p>(a) Convert the offer into an exchange ratio, find the number of new shares, and compute earnings per share after the merger. By how many per cent does Skarv's EPS change? (2 points)</p><p>(b) Compute the merged share price and the NPV of the deal to Skarv's shareholders and to Tind's shareholders. Run the check on the two NPVs. (2 points)</p><p>(c) Skarv's CFO presents the deal as &laquo;double-digit accretive&raquo; and therefore value-creating. In 2–3 sentences, explain why the EPS change is not evidence of value creation, and state the condition under which a stock deal is accretive. (2 points)</p>",
      solution: "<p><b>(a) Exchange ratio and EPS.</b> The offered price per Tind share is 24.00 × 1.25 = 30.00, so ER = 30.00/100.00 = <b>0.3000</b>. New shares x = ER × N<sub>T</sub> = 0.3 × 20 = <b>6.0000</b> million, so the merged firm has N<sub>A</sub> + x = 56.0000 million shares. Before the deal EPS<sub>A</sub> = 250/50 = 5.0000 and EPS<sub>T</sub> = 60/20 = 3.0000. After: EPS<sub>new</sub> = (250 + 60)/56 = 310.00/56 = <b>5.5357</b>. Change: 5.5357/5.0000 − 1 = 0.107143 = <b>+10.7143%</b>. The CFO's slide is arithmetically correct.</p><p><b>(b) Price and NPVs.</b> With S = 0 the merged firm is worth A + T = 50 × 100.00 + 20 × 24.00 = 5000.00 + 480.00 = 5480.00, so P<sub>new</sub> = 5480.00/56 = <b>97.8571 NOK</b>, a fall of 2.1429 per share (−2.1429%). Ownership y = 6/56 = 0.10714286.<br>NPV<sub>A</sub> = (1 − y)(A + T + S) − A = 0.89285714 × 5480.00 − 5000.00 = 4892.8571 − 5000.00 = <b>−107.1429</b><br>NPV<sub>T</sub> = y(A + T + S) − T = 0.10714286 × 5480.00 − 480.00 = 587.1429 − 480.00 = <b>+107.1429</b><br>Check: −107.1429 + 107.1429 = 0.0000 = S ✓ Independent route: NPV<sub>A</sub> = N<sub>A</sub>(P<sub>new</sub> − P<sub>A</sub>) = 50 × (97.8571 − 100.0000) = −107.1429 ✓ So 107.1429 million moved out of Skarv's shareholders' pockets and into Tind's, while EPS rose 10.7%. The same fact seen from the target's side: each Tind share receives 0.3 × 97.8571 = 29.3571, an actual premium of 29.3571/24.00 − 1 = 22.3214% against the offered 25.0000%, because the acquirer overpaid and the currency it pays in fell.</p><p><b>(c) Why accretion is not value creation.</b> Model answer: EPS rises mechanically whenever the acquirer's P/E, 100/5 = 20.0000, exceeds the P/E it pays for the target, 30/3 = 10.0000, because every new share issued buys more earnings than a Skarv share carries; the condition is <b>accretive ⟺ P/E paid for the target &lt; P/E of the acquirer</b>, and it holds here before any analysis of the business. Value is what the market pays for those earnings, and that fell: the merged multiple is 97.8571/5.5357 = 17.6774, down from 20.0000, because Skarv bought slower-growing or riskier earnings that the market capitalises at a lower multiple. The mechanism is the <b>bootstrap (chain-letter) effect</b>: repeat the acquisition every year and reported EPS grows indefinitely without a krone of value being created.</p><p><b>The sharpest proof.</b> Redo the deal at a zero premium, ER = 24/100 = 0.2400: x = 4.8000, total shares 54.8000, EPS<sub>new</sub> = 310.00/54.80 = 5.6569, an accretion of 13.1387%, higher still, while P<sub>new</sub> = 5480.00/54.80 = 100.0000, exactly unchanged ✓ EPS rose 13% in a transaction in which, by construction, nothing was created or moved. An EPS effect that follows from the two multiples alone cannot be evidence about the deal.</p>",
      criteria: [
        "Converts the 25% premium into ER = 0.30 and x = 6 million new shares before touching EPS, then EPS_new = 310/56 = 5.5357, up 10.7143%.",
        "Values the merged firm at the stand-alone sum 5480 with S = 0, so P_new = 97.8571, NPV_A = −107.1429 and NPV_T = +107.1429, and shows that they sum to S = 0.",
        "Says explicitly that EPS accretion and value creation are different things. The classic error is to read the 10.7% EPS rise as a gain to Skarv's shareholders, who in fact lost 107.1429 million.",
        "States the accretion condition: P/E paid (10.0) below the acquirer's own P/E (20.0), equivalently the earnings yield bought (10%) above the acquirer's own (5%).",
        "Names the mechanism, the bootstrap or chain-letter effect, and points to the fall in the combined multiple to 17.6774. Bonus for the zero-premium proof: EPS up 13.1% with the price unchanged at 100.00.",
        "Keeps (c) to 2–3 sentences, as instructed. Length is not rewarded; the named condition and the named mechanism are."
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "k26-3",
      open: true,
      topic: "Implied synergies",
      points: 6,
      body: "<p>Fjordkraft Marine ASA has 30 million shares trading at 210 NOK. It announces an all-cash offer of 68 NOK per share for Sogn Robotikk AS, which has 12 million shares trading at 50 NOK. The bid was not anticipated. On the announcement day Fjordkraft falls to 207.00 and Sogn rises to 68.00. Assume the market expects the deal to close and that nothing else moved the two prices. Figures in millions of NOK.</p><p>(a) What value does the market assign to the synergies of the combination? Show the calculation. (2 points)</p><p>(b) How is that value divided between the two groups of shareholders? Run the consistency check that links the acquirer's gain, the synergies and the premium paid, and say what the result tells you about the price Fjordkraft agreed to pay. (2 points)</p><p>(c) The calculation in (a) rests on assumptions. Name two of them and say, in one sentence each, in which direction the implied synergy is biased if the assumption fails. (2 points)</p>",
      solution: "<p><b>(a) The implied synergy.</b> Whatever the payment method, the two sets of shareholders between them own the whole merged firm, in shares or in cash, so the change in their combined market value is the market's estimate of what the combination adds.<br>Before: A = 30 × 210.00 = 6300.00 and T = 12 × 50.00 = 600.00, combined <b>6900.00</b>.<br>After: 30 × 207.00 = 6210.00 and 12 × 68.00 = 816.00, combined <b>7026.00</b>.<br>S<sub>implied</sub> = 7026.00 − 6900.00 = <b>+126.00</b>. The market believes the combination creates 126 million of value.</p><p><b>(b) Who gets it.</b> Sogn's shareholders receive cash, so their gain is exactly the premium: 12 × (68.00 − 50.00) = <b>+216.00</b>, a 36.0000% premium on the pre-announcement price. Fjordkraft's shareholders gain 30 × (207.00 − 210.00) = <b>−90.00</b>.</p><p><b>Check (independent route):</b> the acquirer's shareholders end up owning A + T + S minus the cash paid out, T + premium, so their gain must be S − premium = 126.00 − 216.00 = −90.00 ✓ The two routes use different prices, so a mis-keyed share count breaks the agreement.</p><p><b>Reading the result.</b> Value created and value captured are two different questions. The combination creates 126.00, but Fjordkraft agreed to pay 216.00 above the target's stand-alone value for it, so it overpaid by 90.00: Sogn's holders capture 216.00/126.00 = 171.43% of the synergy and Fjordkraft's holders −71.43%. The break-even cash offer would have been P<sub>T</sub> + S/N<sub>T</sub> = 50.00 + 126.00/12 = <b>60.5000 NOK</b>; anything above that hands Fjordkraft's shareholders a negative NPV. The negative acquirer return is the signature of overpayment, whether from hubris (the winner's curse) or empire building.</p><p><b>(c) The assumptions, and the direction of the bias.</b> Any two of these earn the points:<br>1. <b>The deal closes with certainty.</b> If the market attaches a probability p &lt; 1 to completion, the observed prices are probability-weighted, and the calculation understates the synergy conditional on closing. (The target would then trade below the 68 offer; here it trades at the offer, which is what makes the assumption plausible.)<br>2. <b>The announcement revealed nothing about the target as a stand-alone business.</b> If the bid signals that Sogn was undervalued, part of its jump is a revaluation rather than a synergy, and S<sub>implied</sub> overstates the gain from combining. The keys reward this caveat explicitly.<br>3. <b>Nothing else moved the prices that day.</b> Market-wide or industry news contaminates the estimate in either direction, which is why event studies net out the market return.</p>",
      criteria: [
        "Uses the change in the combined market value of both firms, 7026 − 6900 = 126, not the target's jump alone (216) and not the acquirer's fall alone.",
        "Identifies the target's gain as the cash premium 12 × 18 = 216 and the acquirer's gain as 30 × (−3) = −90, and runs the check S − premium = 126 − 216 = −90.",
        "Draws the conclusion: the synergy is positive but the acquirer overpaid by 90 million, so value created and value captured are different questions. Bonus for the break-even offer of 60.50.",
        "Names two assumptions and gets the direction right: deal risk makes S_implied understate the synergy conditional on closing; a stand-alone revaluation of the target makes it overstate the gain from combining.",
        "One sentence per assumption, as instructed, and the arithmetic in (a) and (b) shown in full: no calculation, no points."
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "k26-4",
      open: true,
      topic: "Implied deal probability",
      points: 10,
      body: "<p>Company X has announced its intention to acquire company Y. The news came suddenly and there was no anticipation effect. The deal will be completed or will fail in exactly one year from now. If it goes through, 4 shares of Y will be exchanged for 1 share of X. If it fails, both prices return to their pre-announcement levels.</p><p>Share prices before the announcement: P<sub>X,0</sub> = 84, P<sub>Y,0</sub> = 15. Immediately after the announcement: P<sub>X,1</sub> = 80, P<sub>Y,1</sub> = 18.60. The one-year risk-free rate is r<sub>f</sub> = 4%.</p><p>(a) Construct a long-short strategy whose payoff you know in both outcomes. State the position, its net cost today, and its payoff if the deal completes and if it fails. Invest any short-sale proceeds at the risk-free rate. (4 points)</p><p>(b) Using the strategy, calculate the probability that the deal is completed as perceived by the market. Show the equation you solve. (4 points)</p><p>(c) Confirm the probability by an independent route, and state in one sentence what the pair of price reactions says about the deal for X's shareholders. (2 points)</p>",
      solution: "<p><b>(a) The position.</b> First write the ratio as a sentence: 4 shares of Y become 1 share of X, so ER = 0.2500 X-shares per Y-share, and the position that resolves cleanly is long 4 Y against short 1 X. Build it at post-announcement prices:</p><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Leg</th><th>Action</th><th>Cash flow today</th></tr><tr><td>Long target</td><td>Buy 4 shares of Y at 18.60</td><td class=\"n\">−74.40</td></tr><tr><td>Short acquirer</td><td>Short-sell 1 share of X at 80.00</td><td class=\"n\">+80.00</td></tr><tr><td>Risk-free</td><td>Deposit the 80.00 at 4%</td><td class=\"n\">−80.00</td></tr><tr><td><b>Net cost today</b></td><td></td><td class=\"n\"><b>−74.40</b></td></tr></table><p><b>Payoff if the deal completes.</b> The 4 Y shares convert into exactly the 1 X share you owe, which you deliver to close the short. Whatever X is worth then, the two equity legs cancel, and you are left with the deposit: 80.00 × 1.04 = <b>83.2000</b>.</p><p><b>Payoff if the deal fails.</b> No conversion, and prices revert to their pre-announcement levels, so you unwind there: sell 4 Y at 15.00 for +60.00, buy back 1 X at 84.00 for −84.00, and keep the deposit. Payoff = 83.20 + 60.00 − 84.00 = <b>59.2000</b>.</p><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Outcome in one year</th><th>Deposit</th><th>Y leg</th><th>X leg</th><th>Total</th></tr><tr><td>Deal completes (p)</td><td class=\"n\">83.20</td><td colspan=\"2\">4 Y convert into the 1 X owed; legs cancel</td><td class=\"n\"><b>83.2000</b></td></tr><tr><td>Deal fails (1 − p)</td><td class=\"n\">83.20</td><td class=\"n\">+60.00</td><td class=\"n\">−84.00</td><td class=\"n\"><b>59.2000</b></td></tr></table><p><b>(b) The certainty-equivalent equation.</b> The strategy cost 74.40 and its only remaining risk is whether the deal closes, which a regulator or a shareholder vote decides and which is diversifiable, so it carries no risk premium: a fair price requires the expected payoff to equal the cost <i>grown at the risk-free rate</i>, 74.40 × 1.04 = <b>77.3760</b>, not 74.40. This also means the p you solve for is the market's actual belief, not merely a risk-neutral probability as in the binomial model of chapter 22.<br>77.3760 = p(83.2000) + (1 − p)(59.2000)<br>77.3760 − 59.2000 = p(83.2000 − 59.2000)<br>18.1760 = 24.0000 p<br><b>p = 0.757333, that is 75.7333%</b><br>Check: 0.757333 × 83.2000 + 0.242667 × 59.2000 = 63.0101 + 14.3659 = 77.3760 ✓</p><p><b>The two errors that cost the points.</b> Setting the expected payoff equal to 74.40 instead of 77.376 gives 74.40 = 24p + 59.20 and p = 0.6333, and it is the error the H2025 key singles out. Using the post-announcement prices in the failure branch gives a failure payoff of 83.20 + 74.40 − 80.00 = 77.60 and a negative probability, which should stop you: the exercise says prices <i>return to their original levels</i>, so the failure branch uses 84 and 15.</p><p><b>(c) Independent route: the merger spread.</b> One X share minus 4 Y shares is worth 80.00 − 74.40 = 5.6000 today. If the deal completes the spread is worth exactly 0, since 4 Y become 1 X; if it fails it is worth 84.00 − 60.00 = 24.0000. The spread is therefore a pure bet on failure, priced at r<sub>f</sub>: 5.6000 = (1 − p)(24.0000)/1.04, so 1 − p = 5.6000 × 1.04/24.0000 = 5.8240/24.0000 = 0.242667 and <b>p = 0.757333</b> ✓ In words: the spread has narrowed from 24.00 to 5.60, and the ratio of the two widths, grown at r<sub>f</sub>, is the market's failure probability.</p><p><b>Reading the reaction.</b> X fell from 84 to 80 while Y rose from 15 to 18.60, so the market believes the deal destroys value for X's shareholders and transfers it to Y's. You can put a number on it by pricing X under the same p: 1.04 × 80.00 = p × X<sub>s</sub> + (1 − p) × 84.00 gives X<sub>s</sub> = (83.2000 − 20.3840)/0.757333 = 62.8160/0.757333 = <b>82.9437</b> if the deal completes, 1.0563 below where X started, while Y's completion price is 0.25 × 82.9437 = 20.7359, a gain of 38.24% on 15. Verify: [0.757333 × 82.9437 + 0.242667 × 84.00]/1.04 = [62.8160 + 20.3840]/1.04 = 80.0000 ✓ That signature points at empire building or hubris rather than at synergies, which is what the verbal follow-ups in H2025 Exercise 6 asked you to say.</p>",
      criteria: [
        "Gets the direction of the ratio right: 4 shares of Y for 1 share of X means long 4 Y against short 1 X (ER = 0.25). The H2025 key accepts many consistent constructions; internal consistency is what is graded.",
        "Invests the short-sale proceeds at r_f and carries them forward, 80 × 1.04 = 83.20. The success payoff is 83.20 because the equity legs cancel exactly.",
        "Uses the PRE-announcement prices in the failure branch, since the exercise says prices revert: 83.20 − 84 + 60 = 59.20. Using 80 and 18.60 here produces a negative probability.",
        "Sets the expected payoff equal to the cost compounded at r_f, 74.40 × 1.04 = 77.376, not to 74.40. The key calls this out as very important; skipping it gives 63.33% instead of 75.73%.",
        "Writes the equation 77.376 = p(83.2) + (1 − p)(59.2) and solves it to p = 0.7573. Reasonable rounding is accepted; the shown method is not optional.",
        "Confirms p by an independent route (the merger spread 5.60 against 24.00) and reads the reaction: X down, Y up, so value moves from X's holders to Y's. Says why r_f is the right rate: deal risk is diversifiable."
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "k26-5",
      open: true,
      topic: "Merger motives",
      points: 6,
      body: "<p>Three acquisitions are announced on the same day. For each one, give <b>one</b> motive for the deal <b>other than synergies</b> that fits the evidence, name the mechanism by its textbook name, and say whether total value is created, transferred or destroyed and who gains. <b>2–3 sentences per case.</b> &laquo;Cost savings&raquo;, &laquo;economies of scale&raquo; and &laquo;cross-selling&raquo; count as synergies and are not accepted. (2 points each)</p><p>(a) Aksel Industri ASA, a mature firm with large cash holdings and few investment opportunities, whose CEO's pay rises with firm size, announces an all-cash bid for a target in an unrelated industry. Aksel falls 6% on the announcement, the target rises 30%, and the combined market value of the two firms falls.</p><p>(b) Brekke Sement ASA and Cemento Nord AS are the two largest of four producers in a national cement market. Both share prices rise on the announcement, and the competition authority opens an in-depth review the same week.</p><p>(c) Dovre Konsern ASA, which is heavily indebted, buys Eidsvoll Verft AS, whose cash flows are almost uncorrelated with Dovre's. On the announcement the bonds of both firms rise in price, both share prices fall, and the CEO cites &laquo;a more stable earnings profile&raquo; as the rationale.</p>",
      solution: "<p><b>(a) Empire building, an agency cost of free cash flow.</b> A larger firm brings Aksel's CEO a larger salary, more status and more security, so a manager sitting on cash with nothing good to invest it in buys even at a negative NPV; the falling combined market value says total value is <b>destroyed</b>, and the target's 30% premium is a <b>transfer</b> from Aksel's shareholders to the target's. The evidence is the textbook prediction of the free cash flow problem: cash-rich, few opportunities, pay tied to size, negative acquirer return. <i>Accepted alternative with the same signature:</i> managerial hubris and the winner's curse, where the CEO overestimates what the target is worth to him and does not shade his bid for the fact of winning, so the acquirer overpays and the target captures the difference.</p><p><b>(b) Market power.</b> Removing a competitor in a four-firm market lets the combined firm raise prices or squeeze suppliers, so it earns rents; value is <b>created for both sets of shareholders at customers' expense</b>, which is why both prices rise. The catch is the review: rents from market power are precisely what competition authorities exist to block, so the larger the expected rents, the lower the probability the deal closes, and that probability is what the post-announcement prices are already discounting.</p><p><b>(c) Diversification and coinsurance.</b> Pooling two nearly uncorrelated cash-flow streams behind one balance sheet lowers the probability of default, so the debt of both firms becomes safer and the bonds rise; total value is <b>unchanged</b>, and value is <b>transferred from shareholders to creditors</b>, which is why both share prices fall. Consistency check 4 applies: creditors' gain equals shareholders' loss when total value is fixed. The stability the CEO cites is not a shareholder benefit, since shareholders can diversify more cheaply by holding both shares and pay no premium for it; they reclaim the transfer only if the firm relevers afterwards, or through the larger tax shield the extra debt capacity buys.</p><p><b>The repertoire the examiner expects you to hold.</b> H2024 Exercise 1 banned synergies and asked for four reasons in a grid; H2025 Exercise 6 asked for one reason that fits a negative acquirer reaction. Name the mechanism every time.</p><table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Motive</th><th>Mechanism, by name</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Undervaluation</td><td>The acquirer has superior information that the market price understates the target's worth; buying it is a positive-NPV investment with no operating change</td><td>Transfer from the target's selling shareholders to the acquirer; nothing created for society. Needs genuinely superior information</td></tr><tr><td>Empire building</td><td>The free cash flow problem: private benefits of size make managers acquire at a negative NPV when cash is plentiful and governance weak</td><td>Destroyed; the premium is a transfer to the target</td></tr><tr><td>Market power</td><td>Fewer competitors, higher prices or lower input costs, rents</td><td>Created for shareholders at customers' expense; antitrust cuts the probability of closing</td></tr><tr><td>Tax</td><td>Using the target's loss carry-forwards, and higher debt capacity from steadier combined cash flow, which raises PV(TS)</td><td>Transfer from the tax authority; carry-forwards are restricted, and the debt-capacity gain requires actually levering up</td></tr><tr><td>Diversification and coinsurance</td><td>Pooled cash flows default less often, so existing debt becomes safer</td><td>Unchanged in total; transfer from shareholders to creditors (check 4)</td></tr><tr><td>Hubris and the winner's curse</td><td>Bidders estimate with error and the one who overestimated most wins, so the winning bid is biased high; hubris adds a CEO who will not shade for it</td><td>Transfer from the acquirer's shareholders to the target's through overpayment</td></tr><tr><td>Disciplinary takeover</td><td>The market for corporate control: a badly run target is bought at a price reflecting its poor performance and its management replaced</td><td>Created, real operating value; works only if takeover defences do not block it</td></tr></table>",
      criteria: [
        "Gives exactly one motive per case, and none of them is synergies in any disguise: cost savings, economies of scale and cross-selling are all banned by the question.",
        "Names each mechanism by its textbook name: empire building and the free cash flow problem (or hubris and the winner's curse) in (a), market power in (b), coinsurance or diversification in (c).",
        "States for each case whether value is created, transferred or destroyed, and who gains: destroyed with a transfer to the target in (a); created for shareholders at customers' expense in (b); unchanged in total and transferred from shareholders to creditors in (c), citing consistency check 4.",
        "Ties the motive to the evidence given: the fall in combined value in (a), the antitrust review and its effect on the probability of completion in (b), the rising bond prices and falling share prices in (c). Naming a motive without using the price reactions earns little.",
        "Keeps to 2–3 sentences per case. H2025 gives bonus for a well-argued extra observation, for example that Dovre's shareholders could diversify for free by holding both shares, but length alone is not rewarded."
      ]
    }
  ]
};

/* Chapter 27 — Corporate governance */
window.EDU_DATA.chapterTasks[27] = {
  minutes: 79,
  tasks: [
    {
      id: "k27-1",
      open: true,
      topic: "Blockholder actions",
      points: 12,
      body: "<p>Nordlys Media ASA is listed in Oslo and has an equity market value of about 8 billion NOK. Your fund holds 6.5% of the shares and votes, a stake that is public because it crosses the disclosure threshold. No other owner holds more than 3%, and roughly half the register is index funds. Over three years the operating margin has slipped from the peer median to five percentage points below it, while the firm has bought three small companies in unrelated industries at full prices. The annual report shows a leased business jet, a sponsorship of the chief executive's own sailing team and a rebuilt head office. The chief executive also chairs the board, seven of the nine directors have a current or former commercial relationship with the firm, directors sit three-year staggered terms, and the remuneration committee is chaired by the firm's former finance director.</p><p>(a) A colleague argues that the market has known all of this for years, so it sits in the price already and there is nothing left to win. Explain why that reasoning fails, and say what your 6.5% changes relative to a 0.3% index holder and relative to a 55% owner. <b>4&ndash;6 sentences.</b> (3 points)</p><p>(b) Choose <b>three</b> actions you would take. Each must belong to a different governance channel, and each must give the action, the mechanism by which it reaches the chief executive, and one limitation. <b>2&ndash;3 sentences per action.</b> (3 points each)</p>",
      solution: "<p><b>(a) Why &laquo;it is already in the price&raquo; is not an answer.</b> The market does discount the waste by paying less for the shares, but what it is discounting is a deadweight loss and not a transfer: the projects never taken and the krone spent on the jet are gone, and no shareholder receives them. The agency cost is first-best value minus the value the firm reaches under the manager's own objective, and the discount simply decides who paid for it, namely whoever held the shares when the market learned. The investment case is the mirror image of my colleague's point: the discount is priced, the <i>removal</i> of the discount is not, so the improvement accrues to whoever brings it about. At 6.5% I internalise 6.5% of any increase in value, which is enough to clear the cost of a campaign, while a 0.3% index holder collects a rounding error against the same bill and rationally free-rides, which is exactly why nobody has acted in three years. My block is also too large to sell without moving the price, so exit is expensive for me and voice is comparatively cheap, which is the exit-versus-voice trade-off at the stake where it bites. At 55% I would replace the board at the next meeting and none of this would be interesting; at 6.5% every action has to persuade somebody else, and that constraint shapes all three choices below.</p><p><b>Action 1, shareholder voice: requisition an extraordinary general meeting behind a joint slate of directors.</b> Holders of 5% of the share capital can require a general meeting in Norway, so I approach the other institutions, who share my interest and have been free-riding on it, and we put a joint slate of genuinely independent directors and a resolution separating the chair from the chief executive to a recorded vote. The mechanism is that the board is the only body with legal power to set his pay and dismiss him, so changing who sits in the room changes what he faces every day, and directors facing a contested election usually settle before the ballot rather than risk losing the seat. The limitation is that 6.5% cannot carry a resolution alone and the staggered board means a new majority needs two annual cycles, so the campaign lives or dies on the coalition and on patience.</p><p><b>Action 2, internal monitoring: clean out the remuneration committee and rewrite the contract.</b> I demand that the former finance director leave the remuneration committee, that it be filled with directors who have no history with the firm, and that fixed salary be cut in favour of restricted stock with long vesting and a bonus measured against an industry peer group, and I vote against the remuneration report every year until that happens. The mechanism is pay-performance sensitivity: he currently keeps a small fraction of the value he creates and bears almost none of what the jet and the sponsorship cost, so raising that sensitivity makes private benefits expensive for him personally, which is cheaper and more durable than watching him. The limitation is that say-on-pay is advisory in most regimes, so its force is reputational, and sensitivity is not free either, since an undiversified manager must be compensated for bearing firm-specific risk and option-heavy pay invites risk shifting.</p><p><b>Action 3, the market for corporate control: use the stake as a toehold.</b> I tell the board that if the margin gap is not closed I will support a change of control, and I approach industrial buyers and financial sponsors with my 6.5% as a committed toehold, or lead a take-private with a sponsor myself. The mechanism is that a hostile bid needs nobody's permission: it is made to shareholders over the board's head and replaces the whole team at once, and the gap between what the firm is worth under this chief executive and under a competent one is itself the invitation, so even the threat disciplines. The limitation is that it needs a willing buyer, that a staggered board combined with a poison pill can make a hostile purchase practically impossible without board consent, and that the free-rider problem in takeovers forces the bidder to hand most of the improvement to selling shareholders in the premium.</p><p><b>A fourth if there is time, and H2025 pays for creativity.</b> Demand a special dividend or a leveraged recapitalisation: cash inside the firm is what funds the jet and the unrelated acquisitions, whereas interest and principal are contractual, default would end his career, and every new project then has to be priced by outside investors. The cost of that remedy belongs in the same breath, since leverage brings distress costs and the shareholder-creditor conflicts of overhang and risk shifting.</p><p><b>Naming the mechanism.</b> Say out loud which side of the system each action sits on, because the examiner marks the channel as well as the action: Action 1 is the shareholder's own toolkit, voting and meeting rights used to change the board; Action 2 is internal monitoring, working through the board's remuneration committee; Action 3 is the market for corporate control; the bonus fourth is the free cash flow channel, changing the balance sheet rather than the people. Three actions from three channels cannot be read as one action written three times, which is the trap in this question.</p>",
      criteria: [
        "Exactly three actions in (b), each explained in 2-3 sentences, each with the action, the mechanism reaching the CEO and a limitation. The three are graded independently, so three thin but correct actions beat one long essay.",
        "The 6.5% stake is used in the argument: large enough that monitoring pays and that the 5% meeting-requisition and disclosure thresholds are crossed, too small to carry a vote alone, and too large to exit cheaply. An answer that would read identically for a 0.3% holder loses most of (a).",
        "Part (a) says why market pricing does not dissolve the problem: the waste is a deadweight loss, not a transfer, and it is the earlier owners who paid for it. Credit for the corollary that the removal of the discount is not priced.",
        "Each action names its governance channel: internal monitoring, the shareholder's own toolkit, the market for corporate control, or the free cash flow channel. Naming the channel is worth as much as describing the action.",
        "Three actions from the same channel count as one action written three times. Voting against the directors and running a proxy contest to replace those directors are the standard collision; so are engaging the board and asking the board for a seat.",
        "Any reasonable, well-explained action is accepted, including litigation, demanding an independent investigation or simply selling the block, provided the mechanism is explained. Sentence budgets are enforced; length alone earns nothing."
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "k27-2",
      open: true,
      topic: "The small institutional owner",
      points: 8,
      body: "<p>You run stewardship at a Norwegian pension fund. The fund holds 0.5% of Vestfjord Sj&oslash;mat ASA, one of roughly 900 positions in a broad index mandate: the mandate does not allow you to sell an index constituent, your team has six people for the whole portfolio, and your annual engagement budget for all 900 holdings is smaller than the legal bill for a single contested director election. Vestfjord's chief executive has missed the announced cost programme three years running, has stopped attending the capital markets day, and has hired his former partner as head of strategy. The chair is a personal friend of his. Last year the remuneration report passed with 71% support, and the largest shareholder in the register holds 2.8%.</p><p>(a) Name <b>two</b> actions that are still worth taking from a 0.5% position that cannot be sold. For each, give the action, the mechanism by which it reaches the chief executive, and why its cost is low enough to be worth bearing at this stake. <b>2&ndash;3 sentences each.</b> (2 points each)</p><p>(b) Name <b>two</b> actions from the standard shareholder repertoire that you should <b>not</b> take here, and say in one sentence each why they fail at 0.5%. (2 points)</p><p>(c) State the condition under which any shareholder finds monitoring privately worthwhile, and explain what changes when the holder is forbidden to sell. <b>3&ndash;4 sentences.</b> (2 points)</p>",
      solution: "<p><b>(a) Two actions that survive the cost test.</b></p><p><b>Action 1, vote the block and publish the reason.</b> I vote against the re-election of the chair and of the nomination committee, vote against the remuneration report, and publish a short rationale for each vote on the fund's website before the meeting. The mechanism is reputational rather than legal: directors and executives care intensely about a visible protest vote, because it is read by the labour market for directors and by the proxy advisers whose recommendations move the rest of the register, and last year's 71% is already close to the level at which a Norwegian board feels obliged to respond. The cost test passes because voting is a fixed cost the fund already pays across 900 holdings: the marginal cost of this vote is a few hours, so even 0.5% of a modest improvement clears it.</p><p><b>Action 2, join a collective engagement with the other institutions.</b> I write to the six or seven institutional holders who together own perhaps a quarter of the register and propose a joint letter to the chair with one specific demand, an independent chair and a published cost plan with dates, and a stated intention to vote together if it is refused. The mechanism is that the pooled stake, not mine, is what the board reacts to, and a coalition speaking to the board reaches the chief executive through the only body that can set his pay and remove him. The cost test passes because the cost is shared while the improvement is internalised by the group: I am buying a share of the campaign rather than the whole of it, which is the only way a 0.5% holder ever gets over the free-rider threshold.</p><p><b>(b) Two actions to leave alone.</b> A proxy contest to replace the board: the activist pays the entire legal, advisory and solicitation bill and keeps 0.5% of the gain, so the arithmetic is hopeless, and this is the textbook public good that dispersed owners never supply. Selling the stake, the Wall Street walk: the mandate forbids it, so the threat that disciplines a discretionary holder is not available to me at all, and management knows it. <i>Either of these is accepted, and so are two others:</i> requisitioning an extraordinary general meeting, because the Norwegian threshold is 5% of the share capital and I am nowhere near it; and litigation, which needs evidence of a breach of duty rather than mere poor performance and is slow and expensive even when the evidence exists.</p><p><b>(c) The condition, and what the sale ban does to it.</b> A holder acts when the value of voice exceeds its cost, that is when the probability of success times his fraction of the firm times the improvement exceeds the full cost of acting, so the break-even stake is the cost divided by the improvement. At 0.5% that condition is met only by actions whose cost is close to zero or whose cost is shared, which is precisely why the two actions in (a) are a vote and a coalition and not a campaign. Being unable to sell removes the other half of the classical trade-off: exit versus voice collapses into voice or nothing, so the implicit threat to depress the price by leaving, which disciplines a discretionary owner, is worthless here. That is why index investors have built cheap, scalable voice, published voting policies, proxy advisers and collective engagement platforms, instead of expensive bespoke voice, and why their weakness is a monitoring budget per holding that is tiny even though their aggregate voting power is enormous.</p><p><b>Naming the mechanism.</b> Both surviving actions belong to the shareholder's own toolkit, but they work through different channels: the vote reaches the chief executive through internal monitoring, since it is the board that must react to it, while the coalition manufactures a blockholder out of dispersed owners and so attacks the free-rider problem itself. The market for corporate control and exit, the two channels a genuine blockholder would reach for, are both closed to a small index holder, and saying so explicitly is part of the answer.</p>",
      criteria: [
        "Both actions in (a) are genuinely cheap or genuinely shared: voting and publishing the rationale, supporting someone else's proposal, collective engagement with other institutions, or working through the proxy advisers. An action that costs real money fails the test at 0.5%.",
        "The 0.5% stake and the no-sell mandate are used in the argument, not just mentioned. An answer that would read identically for a 7% activist fund earns little.",
        "Part (b) names two actions that fail and says why: a proxy contest or a take-private because the cost falls on one holder who keeps 0.5% of the gain, exit because the mandate forbids it, an EGM because 5% of the capital is required in Norway, litigation because it needs a breach of duty rather than weak performance.",
        "Part (c) states the monitoring condition, stake times improvement against cost, and the break-even stake that follows, and says that a sale ban collapses the exit-versus-voice choice into voice or nothing.",
        "Each action names its channel: internal monitoring, the shareholder's own toolkit, or the market for corporate control, and notes that the last one is unavailable at this size.",
        "Sentence budgets are enforced: 2-3 sentences per action in (a), one sentence each in (b), 3-4 sentences in (c). Several short correct answers beat one long one, and two actions that are the same action twice, for example voting against the chair and voting against the pay report, count once."
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "k27-3",
      open: true,
      topic: "Takeover defences",
      points: 10,
      body: "<p>Ranheim Instrument ASA trades at 254 NOK. An industrial competitor announces an unsolicited all-cash offer of 340 NOK per share, a premium of 34%, and states that it intends to replace the management team. Within 48 hours, and without soliciting a competing offer, the board rejects the bid as opportunistic, adopts a shareholder rights plan with a 15% trigger, and announces that it will ask the next annual general meeting to replace the annual election of all directors with three classes serving three-year terms. The rights plan was adopted by board resolution alone; no shareholder voted on it.</p><p>Other facts on the record: the operating margin has been below the peer median for four years and the return on invested capital has been below the firm's cost of capital for three; the chief executive owns 0.3% of the shares and has a change-of-control payment worth three years of pay; five of the nine directors have served more than twelve years; the chair is a former chief executive of the firm.</p><p>(a) Explain how the rights plan works and why, once it is in force, the decision whether Ranheim is sold sits with the board rather than with the owners of the shares. Say what the proposed classified board adds to it. <b>4&ndash;6 sentences.</b> (4 points)</p><p>(b) Name the two competing hypotheses about defences, state what each predicts that the other does not, and say which one the facts here support. <b>4&ndash;6 sentences.</b> (4 points)</p><p>(c) You hold 4% and believe this is entrenchment. Name <b>two</b> concrete things you can do before the annual meeting, one sentence each. (2 points)</p>",
      solution: "<p><b>(a) The mechanism, and where the power actually sits.</b> The plan gives every shareholder except the bidder the right to buy newly issued shares at a deep discount the moment the bidder's holding passes 15%, so crossing the trigger multiplies the share count and dilutes the bidder's own stake to a fraction of what he paid for. No rational bidder ever triggers a pill, which is why pills are almost never exercised and almost always effective: the threat alone makes an open-market or tender-offer purchase unthinkable. The decisive detail is that only the board can redeem the plan, so the bid does not disappear, it is redirected from the shareholders to the boardroom, and the question of whether the owners may sell their own property is answered by the nine people the sale would displace. The classified board is what turns a delay into a barrier: with all directors elected annually, a bidder who wins the argument with shareholders can replace the board at one meeting and have the pill redeemed within the year, whereas three classes mean he must win two consecutive annual meetings, which is longer than almost any bidder or any financing commitment will wait. Pill plus staggered board is therefore the most effective entrenchment structure known, and the two must be assessed together rather than one at a time.</p><p><b>(b) The two hypotheses and the evidence that separates them.</b> Under the <b>shareholder-bargaining hypothesis</b> the pill is a negotiating instrument: it replaces a dispersed, free-riding shareholder base with a single negotiator, lets the board defeat a coercive low-ball or two-tier tender, and buys time to run an auction, so protected firms should receive <i>higher-priced</i> bids and those eventually sold should sell for more. Under the <b>managerial-entrenchment hypothesis</b> the pill protects jobs: it lets directors refuse an offer shareholders would have accepted, so protected firms should receive <i>fewer</i> bids and should show weaker operating performance, because the discipline of the takeover threat has been removed. The two are not distinguishable from the board's own description of its motives, which is why the test has to be in the data and in the firm's conduct. Here the facts point one way: four years of below-peer margins and three years of returns below the cost of capital are the operating signature of the entrenchment story, and the board rejected a 34% premium in 48 hours without soliciting a single competing offer, which is the opposite of what a negotiator seeking a higher price would do. The negotiator is also not independent of the negotiation, since the chair is a former chief executive and five directors have sat for over twelve years, and while the change-of-control payment removes part of the chief executive's personal reason to resist, three years of pay set against a 0.3% shareholding means his stake in keeping the job still dwarfs his stake in the share price. The honest conclusion is conditional: a pill is defensible as a temporary negotiating instrument with a sunset and a shareholder vote behind it, and indefensible as a permanent device adopted unilaterally by an entrenched board that is refusing to run a process.</p><p><b>(c) Two things to do before the meeting.</b> Any two of these:</p><p>1. File a shareholder proposal requiring the rights plan to be ratified by the general meeting and to lapse after twelve months unless renewed by a vote, and campaign publicly for it so the proxy advisers pick it up. 2. Vote against the classified-board resolution and against the re-election of the longest-serving directors and the chair, and say publicly and in advance that you will do so, since a large announced protest vote often moves a board before the ballot. 3. Build a coalition with the other institutional holders and requisition a general meeting, since 4% plus one more holder clears the Norwegian 5% threshold. 4. Tell the bidder publicly that your 4% will support a proxy contest, which is the one route to the pill that does not need the incumbent board's consent. 5. Where the 48-hour rejection can be shown to be a breach of the directors' duty of care, threaten a derivative claim, keeping in mind that poor judgement is much harder to litigate than a conflict of interest.</p><p><b>Naming the mechanism.</b> This question sits entirely in the market for corporate control, and a defence is that channel with the sign reversed: the hostile bid is the monitor of last resort precisely because it needs no one's permission, and the pill restores the permission requirement. The remedies in (c) come from the other two channels, the shareholder's own toolkit in the proposal, the votes and the proxy contest, and internal monitoring in the demand for an independent chair and a genuine sale process.</p>",
      criteria: [
        "Part (a) gets the mechanism right: every holder except the bidder buys discounted shares once the trigger is crossed, the bidder is diluted, and only the board can redeem the plan, so the effect is a board veto over the shareholders' own decision to sell.",
        "Part (a) explains what the classified board adds: a bidder must win two consecutive annual meetings to replace the board and redeem the pill, so the two defences together are far stronger than either alone.",
        "Part (b) names both hypotheses, shareholder bargaining and managerial entrenchment, and states a prediction each makes that the other does not: higher premiums and higher eventual sale prices against fewer bids and weaker operating performance. Answering yes or no without both hypotheses caps the score.",
        "Part (b) applies the evidence in the case rather than reciting theory: four years below the peer margin, a 48-hour rejection with no competing offer solicited, a former chief executive as chair and five directors with more than twelve years of tenure. The 0.3% holding against three years of parachute pay is a bonus observation.",
        "The conclusion is conditional and carries a remedy, for example ratification by the general meeting and a sunset clause, rather than a flat verdict.",
        "Sentence budgets are respected, 4-6 sentences in (a) and (b) and one sentence per item in (c), and two remedies that are the same remedy twice, such as voting against the directors and running a proxy fight against those directors, count once."
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "k27-4",
      open: true,
      topic: "Compensation design",
      points: 8,
      body: "<p>You sit on the nomination committee of Troms&oslash; Diagnostikk ASA, a listed firm whose value depends almost entirely on research programmes that pay off five to ten years out. The chief executive's contract reads as follows.</p><p>Fixed salary of 9.5 million NOK, which is 82% of his expected total pay. An annual cash bonus of up to 40% of salary, paid on reported EBITDA against a budget that the chief executive himself proposes to the board each autumn. Options over 1.2% of the shares, struck at the money, vesting after twelve months and freely sellable on the day they vest. He holds no shares outright and is under no obligation to hold any. There is no clawback, and last year's bonus was paid on figures that the auditor required the firm to restate four months later. The bonus was paid in full in a year in which the entire sector index rose 40% on a change in public reimbursement rates. The remuneration committee has three members, two of whom are former executives of the firm, and the chief executive attends its meetings.</p><p>(a) Identify <b>three</b> distinct weaknesses in this contract. For each, name the mechanism that is broken and propose one concrete replacement. <b>2&ndash;3 sentences each.</b> (2 points each)</p><p>(b) A colleague concludes that the fix is simply to give the chief executive as much equity as possible. Explain why maximum pay-performance sensitivity is not the objective. <b>2&ndash;3 sentences.</b> (2 points)</p>",
      solution: "<p><b>(a) Three weaknesses, three replacements.</b></p><p><b>1. The sensitivity is close to zero, and it is the sensitivity that matters, not the level.</b> Pay-performance sensitivity is the change in the manager's wealth per krone of change in firm value, and a fixed salary contributes exactly nothing to it, so with 82% of expected pay fixed and no shareholding at all this chief executive keeps a negligible fraction of any value he creates and bears almost none of what he wastes. The replacement is to cut fixed pay sharply and deliver the difference in restricted shares, together with a minimum shareholding requirement of several times salary that he must reach and maintain. The mechanism is that raising his sensitivity makes shirking and private benefits expensive for him personally, which works without anyone having to observe his effort and is therefore cheaper and more durable than monitoring.</p><p><b>2. The horizon is twelve months in a business whose payoffs are ten years out.</b> Options that vest in a year and can be sold the same day reward whatever moves the share price before the next summer, which in a research-driven firm means cutting the very programmes that create the value, and they also give him a one-year reason to manage reported earnings rather than a ten-year reason to build the pipeline. The replacement is vesting over five years or more, a requirement to hold the shares for a further period after vesting, and deferral of a large part of the bonus. The mechanism is horizon matching: the instrument should pay out when the consequences of the decision are visible, not before.</p><p><b>3. The bonus pays for the sector, on a number the chief executive sets himself, with nothing to reverse it.</b> Paying a full bonus in a year when the whole index rose 40% on a reimbursement decision rewards a systematic move he did not create, while letting him propose the EBITDA budget invites him to sandbag it and to manage the reported figure, as the restatement shows, and without a clawback the money paid on figures later reversed simply stays paid. The replacement is relative performance evaluation against a defined peer group, a metric the committee sets and that is harder to manipulate than reported EBITDA, and a clawback that bites on restated results. The mechanism is the separation of systematic from firm-specific performance: he should be paid for the part he controls, not for the part every holder of the index received anyway.</p><p><b>A fourth, if the question allowed one, and the one an examiner will reward for creativity:</b> the process itself is captured, since two of three committee members are the firm's own former executives and the person whose pay is being set sits in the room. Replace them with directors who have no history with the firm, have the committee retain its own adviser, exclude the chief executive from the discussion of his own contract, and put the remuneration report to an annual say-on-pay vote so that a protest vote carries a public reputational cost.</p><p><b>(b) Why not maximum sensitivity.</b> A chief executive cannot diversify his human capital: his job, his reputation and now his wealth all ride on one firm, so loading him with equity forces him to bear firm-specific risk that shareholders have already diversified away, and he must be paid extra to accept it, which makes high-sensitivity pay genuinely expensive. He will also respond to that undiversified exposure by declining risky positive-NPV projects that shareholders want taken, while a heavy option package pushes him the other way, because a convex payoff makes risk shifting attractive to him at shareholders' and creditors' expense. The optimum is therefore interior, and the real design question is not how much sensitivity but sensitivity to what: relative performance, long vesting, and a mix of restricted stock and options.</p><p><b>Naming the mechanism.</b> Everything in this question belongs to internal monitoring, because the board's remuneration committee is the body that writes the contract, but note the two escalation routes into the shareholder's own toolkit when the committee will not act: voting against the remuneration report at the say-on-pay vote, and filing a shareholder proposal on the pay structure. Compensation is the governance tool that changes what the manager wants, where every other mechanism in the chapter only changes what he can get away with.</p>",
      criteria: [
        "Three distinct weaknesses, each with a named mechanism and a concrete replacement, in 2-3 sentences each. A weakness identified without a replacement, or a replacement without the mechanism, earns part marks only.",
        "The mechanisms are named in the vocabulary the keys use: pay-performance sensitivity, horizon and vesting, relative performance evaluation, clawback, earnings management, committee independence. Accepted weaknesses include the 82% fixed pay, the twelve-month vesting and free sale, the self-proposed EBITDA target, the absent peer benchmark, the missing clawback after a restatement, the option-only convexity, and the captured committee.",
        "Three weaknesses that are the same weakness twice count once: the missing clawback and the restated bonus are one point, and short vesting and free sale on vesting are one point unless the answer separates them explicitly.",
        "The horizon argument is tied to this firm: a ten-year research pipeline paid on a twelve-month instrument is what makes the mismatch a real weakness rather than a general complaint.",
        "Part (b) gives the reason maximum sensitivity is wrong: the manager is undiversified, must be compensated for firm-specific risk, and will avoid risk shareholders want taken, while options overshoot into risk shifting. The optimum is interior and the question is sensitivity to what.",
        "Sentence budgets are respected. No calculation is expected anywhere in this exercise; a purely verbal answer scores full marks."
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "k27-5",
      open: true,
      topic: "The LBO as governance",
      points: 6,
      body: "<p>Hallingdal Verkt&oslash;y ASA makes hand tools. The business is mature, grows with the building cycle and no faster, and throws off steady cash: the firm holds 1.4 billion NOK of cash, carries no debt, and its share price has gone nowhere for six years. In that period the chief executive has bought a regional airline and a small hotel chain, neither of which has earned its cost of capital, and the register is dispersed with no holder above 3%.</p><p>A private equity sponsor takes the firm private at a 32% premium, funding the purchase with about 70% debt and putting up the rest as equity. The existing management team is kept on and given 9% of the new equity, vesting over five years and realisable only on a sale or a relisting. The new board has five members, three of them partners of the sponsor, and it meets monthly.</p><p>(a) Name and explain <b>four</b> distinct mechanisms by which this transaction disciplines the chief executive. <b>2 sentences each.</b> (1 point each)</p><p>(b) Name <b>two</b> costs the same structure creates, say who bears each, and connect each to the relevant capital structure material. One sentence each. (2 points)</p>",
      solution: "<p><b>(a) Four mechanisms, and they operate simultaneously.</b></p><p><b>1. Ownership is concentrated, so the free-rider problem disappears by construction.</b> A dispersed register with no holder above 3% supplies no monitoring, because each owner pays the whole cost of intervening and keeps a sliver of the gain, which is why six flat years and two bad acquisitions went unchallenged. The sponsor owns the entire equity, so it internalises 100% of any improvement and monitoring is worth its cost automatically, which is what a board of three sponsor partners meeting monthly actually is.</p><p><b>2. Debt strips out the free cash flow that funded the empire.</b> Interest and principal on a structure financed 70% by debt are contractual and must be paid on schedule, so the cash that bought an airline and a hotel chain is committed before the chief executive can reach it, and this is the agency benefit of leverage rather than a financing decision. It also forces him back to the capital market for anything new, where each project is priced by outsiders instead of being funded quietly from the balance sheet.</p><p><b>3. Default is a personal penalty outside his control, which is what makes the commitment credible.</b> Missing a payment hands the firm to creditors and ordinarily ends the manager's career, so the threat is severe, automatic and not something a friendly board can waive. A promise to be disciplined is cheap talk; a debt schedule is the same promise with a bailiff behind it.</p><p><b>4. Management equity raises pay-performance sensitivity by an order of magnitude.</b> Nine per cent of the equity, vesting over five years and realisable only on an exit, means the team keeps a large and long-dated fraction of every krone of value it creates and bears the same fraction of what it wastes, so private benefits become expensive for the people enjoying them. Delisting reinforces this: there are no quarterly earnings to manage and no dispersed register to coordinate, so the horizon of the incentive matches the horizon of the decisions.</p><p><b>(b) Two costs.</b> The leverage brings expected financial distress costs, direct and indirect, and they are borne by whoever holds the claims today, since they are anticipated and priced into the terms the sponsor gets, exactly as the trade-off theory sets out. It also opens the shareholder-creditor conflicts of a highly levered firm, risk shifting and debt overhang, where the sponsor's equity now has a convex payoff that makes gambling attractive and makes positive-NPV projects that mainly rescue the creditors unattractive, with the cost falling on lenders ex post and on the sponsor ex ante through the price of the debt. <i>A third, also accepted:</i> the agency problem simply moves up a level, because the sponsor's own investors cannot monitor the sponsor, and a fourth is that a business with lumpy or cyclical investment needs is harmed by losing the cash buffer that the discipline removes.</p><p><b>Naming the mechanism.</b> The buyout is the market for corporate control acting through the balance sheet rather than through a vote, and it is the one transaction that fixes all three governance channels at once: it replaces the dispersed register with a single monitoring owner, rebuilds internal monitoring in a small active board, and removes the manager's discretion over cash. That is also why the same instrument appears twice in this course with opposite signs: debt as the agency benefit that disciplines managers, and debt as the agency cost that turns shareholders against creditors.</p>",
      criteria: [
        "Four genuinely distinct mechanisms, not four descriptions of leverage. The standard four are concentrated ownership, the cash-flow discipline of debt, the career and control consequences of default, and high-powered management equity; delisting and the small active board are accepted as a fifth.",
        "Concentrated ownership is tied to the free-rider problem: the old register had no holder above 3% and therefore supplied no monitoring, while the sponsor internalises the whole improvement.",
        "The debt argument names both channels, cash and control: contractual payments remove the free cash flow that funded the airline and the hotels, and default ends the manager's career, which is what makes the commitment credible rather than cheap talk.",
        "Part (b) names two real costs and who bears them, and connects them to the capital structure material: expected distress costs under the trade-off theory, and risk shifting and debt overhang as the shareholder-creditor conflicts of high leverage. The agency problem at the sponsor's own level is accepted as a bonus.",
        "Sentence budgets are respected, 2 sentences per mechanism and one per cost. Four short correct mechanisms beat two long ones, and no calculation is required anywhere."
      ]
    }
  ]
};
